// 4 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("4  Vessel configuration — the single source of truth"));
c.push(P("This chapter describes the architectural feature that most distinguishes this console: it studies ASV behavior across vessel TYPES, so no vessel parameter may be hardcoded anywhere."));
c.push(P("Every hull, windage, propulsion, maneuvering, autopilot, power, planning and spawn value for a modeled vessel lives in exactly one place — `vessels/<id>.json`. The server validates it at load, publishes it to module globals, and serves it to the client at `GET /api/vessel`. Server and client therefore agree by construction."));
c.push(H2("4.1  Lifecycle"));
c.push(B("`load_vessel(id)` reads `vessels/<id>.json`."));
c.push(B("`validate_vessel(v)` checks it against a field schema. A missing or mistyped field fails the load with a clear, path-pointed error — a bad file never runs with placeholder physics."));
c.push(B("`apply_vessel(v)` publishes the values to the module globals that `SimVcu`, the mission store and the planners read."));
c.push(B("The client's `loadVessel()` fetches `/api/vessel` and mirrors the same values into its own constants (speeds, turn rate, nogo buffer, minimum navigable depth, search-pattern sizes) and the energy gauge."));
c.push(P("`--vessel <id>` selects the vessel at start. `POST /api/vessel {id}` switches it live; the switch is gated on disarmed and idle, returns 409 otherwise, and respawns the simulated vessel at the new vessel's spawn point."));
c.push(H2("4.2  The staleness rule"));
c.push(P("EVERY value derived from the vessel must be re-derived inside `apply_vessel()`. A constant computed once at import goes stale the moment the operator switches vessel in the top-bar picker, and the resulting bug is subtle — the console keeps running, with one subsystem quietly modeling the previous hull. Historic instances: the underwater lateral area used for leeway drag, and the ROC recovery standoff."));
c.push(H2("4.3  Schema"));
c.push(TBL(["Block", "Fields", "Drives"], [
  ["`hull`", "`loa_m`, `beam_m`, `above_water_h_m`, `draft_m`, `wind_cd`, `hull_cd`, optional `air_draft_m`, `wind_area_side_m2`, `wind_area_front_m2` and `underwater_lateral_area_m2`", "Windage silhouettes, leeway drag, minimum navigable depth, ROC recovery standoff"],
  ["`propulsion`", "`speeds_kn.{low,survey,high}`", "The console's own speeds (the guard's low, the escape's high, the hold's ladder), the whole knots the operator selects from (low rounded up to high rounded down, derived at load and published as `speed_steps_kn`), ETA estimates, turn radius, moving-HOME closing check"],
  ["`maneuvering`", "`max_turn_rate_deg_s`, `approach_m`, `lookahead_m`, `arrival_radius_m`", "Minimum turn radius, survey turn geometry, waypoint following"],
  ["`autopilot`", "`xte_ki_deg`, `xte_i_max_deg`", "Cross-track integral term under a steady sideways push"],
  ["`power`", "`type` = `battery` (voltage sag) or `fuel` (liters, burn curve)", "Endurance, range, the console's energy gauge"],
  ["`planning`", "`nogo_buffer_m`, `under_keel_clearance_m`, optional `overhead_clearance_m`, `channel_reach_m`, optional `min_survey_line_m`, `roc`, `search`", "Keep-out buffer, depth floor, channel lane reach, shortest survey line worth running for this hull, ROC defaults, search sizes"],
  ["`display`", "optional `hull_color`, `hull_color2`", "The vessel marker's color on the chart - the hull's own livery. Absent falls back to the chart default, so an older profile is unchanged"],
  ["`spawn`", "`lat`, `lon`", "Where the simulated vessel comes up WHEN NO OPERATING PORT IS SELECTED - a port, if there is one, decides position (see 4.5)"],
], [1500, 3700, 4160]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("The minimum navigable depth is DERIVED, not declared: `draft_m + under_keel_clearance_m`. A deep-draft vessel therefore treats more water as nogo automatically, and the client re-extracts the chart on a vessel switch so the keep-out model follows."));
c.push(P("The clearance she needs OVERHEAD is derived the same way (12 October 2026): `air_draft_m + overhead_clearance_m` (chart.js airNeedM). A bridge span or an overhead line charted lower than that, or with no clearance charted, is a keep-out; an opening bridge is judged shut. The DriX carries 2.85 m (off iXblue's drawing, both gondolas), the 4 m example 1.97 m (off its general arrangement), the small-class boat 1.1 m (its user guide's whole height), each with 1.0 m of overhead clearance. A profile without `air_draft_m` judges nothing overhead; a malformed one is refused at load."));
c.push(P("A HULL'S AREAS ARE MEASURED WHERE IT NEEDS THEM (9 October 2026). The wind force acts on the side area times the sine of the apparent wind's angle off the bow plus the front area times its cosine, and the leeway is the speed at which the water's drag on the underwater lateral area balances it. The areas used to be one above-water height times the length or the beam, and the length times the draft, which describe neither a long low hull under a tall narrow mast nor a 2.0 m draft that is a gondola on a slender drop keel. Measured off iXblue's own dimensioned starboard view of the DriX (in the DriX training notes: the design waterline, the base line and a 7,667 mm length chain; scaled by the chain, it reproduces the drawing's 4.85 m height and 2 m draft), the wind sees 6.2 square meters side-on, 6.5 with the H-8's radar dome and stern pole, and about 1.8 end-on; the water sees 4.2 below the design waterline - 3.1 for the hull, 1.1 for the keel strut, gondola, rudder and skeg - where the length times the draft said 15.4, so every DriX leeway ran about 1.9 times too slow. (A first measurement from photographs, the same evening, had the side at 4.9: about a quarter low.) So a hull file may give `wind_area_side_m2`, `wind_area_front_m2` and `underwater_lateral_area_m2`; `validate_vessel` refuses each unless it is a positive number, `apply_vessel` uses them, and a hull that gives none keeps the old rules. The DriX's `wind_source` and `underwater_source` record the measurement and its ranges. With them a stopped DriX drifts 1.90 times faster beam-on than head-on, where one height made it 3.06, and in a steady beam wind about 4.4 percent of the wind's speed."));
c.push(P("THE DRIX'S TWO GONDOLAS (9 October 2026). The DriX carries one of two gondolas, each sized round the sonar in it, and the gondola sets her draft, her underwater area, her drag and so her top speed, burn, coast and slow-down - so there are two profiles of the one hull. `drix08` is the original Kongsberg EM2040 gondola (iXblue drawing 920C12-1-DWG-002), 2.03 m long and 0.31 m deep, the one in iXblue's general drawing and fitted for every log measurement: draft 2.0 m, underwater 4.2 square meters, top speed 11.5 kn on its measured speed law, and its measured fuel law, coast and slow-down. `drix08_em712` is the EM712 gondola (922C01-DWG-002: 2246.7 by 555.8 by 1207.9 mm, 577 kg in air), fitted now and the console's default: the same keel puts its bottom 0.25 m deeper (draft 2.25 m, depth floor 3.15 m), its 0.73 square-meter silhouette replaces the EM2040's 0.47 (underwater 4.5), its top speed is 10 kn at 3100 rpm (Andy's own figure; the 2024 speed law reads 9.3 there) and its fuel law the 2024 trials'. Both burn curves are fitted to the Fuel planner's measured laws - the EM2040's within 3 percent from 7 to 11 kn - where they had been fitted to Exail's published endurances, which had the DriX burning 1.65 L/h at 7 kn against the 2.61 measured. The EM712's coast and slow-down are not yet measured; they are the EM2040's scaled by mass over drag (1.43 times the drag, from the fuel per hour at 8 kn; about 9 percent more mass), each marked measured: false. Each profile records its gondola under hull.gondola and the provenance of every number in its source fields."));
c.push(H2("4.5  Operating ports (ports.json)"));
c.push(P("A PORT IS WHERE YOU ARE; THE VESSEL IS WHAT YOU ARE DRIVING. They were one thing until the port registry existed: every vessel file carried its own spawn, so choosing a hull chose a location, and the console could not be pointed at another base without editing a vessel's configuration. Ports are now their own registry, in the same shape as the vessel picker - a list, an active id, and a switch permitted only while disarmed and idle, because moving the base under a running boat is as incoherent as swapping its physics."));
c.push(TBL(["Field", "Value", "Drives"], [
  ["`id`", "stable key, derived from the name when absent", "What `--base` and the picker refer to"],
  ["`name`", "display name", "What the operator reads in the dropdown"],
  ["`lat` / `lon`", "decimal degrees", "The chart's opening view, and where the simulated vessel spawns"],
  ["`ofs`", "optional NOAA Operational Forecast System id", "Which regional current model covers this base. A model is REGIONAL: the wrong one answers with an error rather than a current. Left blank, the base gets the model named by --currents-ofs, or none - never the previous port's, which a blank port kept until 9 October 2026"],
  ["`note`", "free text", "Why this position and not another - siting, charted depth, known limits"],
], [1500, 3700, 4160]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("The active port has the last word on the spawn: apply_port() runs at the end of apply_vessel(), so a hull's own spawn is only the fallback for a console with no port selected. A vessel switch therefore does NOT move the boat, and a port switch does."));
c.push(P("A port can be created FROM A NAME. Posting a name with no position geocodes it, and then - the half that matters - snaps the result to charted water deep enough for the selected hull. A geocoder returns the center of a TOWN, and a town center is on land: a well known Alaskan port resolves to a street corner, and the console walks outward through the chart's own depth areas until it finds a berth the vessel can float in. Where no navigable water is charted nearby the port is still created at the place center, but flagged unverified and carrying the reason - never presented as a berth. A name matching nothing is refused rather than becoming a port at sea."));
c.push(P("Ports the operator adds are RETAINED. The picker's add entry saves the chart's current center under a typed name and writes it back to the registry, so a base entered once is in the list from then on - the gesture carries the point, exactly as the chart menu's Go-To, Set Home and Spawn do, rather than asking anyone to read a latitude off the screen and type it back in."));
c.push(P("A suite that touches ports MUST keep the registry out of the application directory - every suite that starts a console now gives it a state folder of its own with --state-dir, which moves ports.json with the rest of its state; --ports-config still names a single file. Switching or adding SAVES, so a test run against the application directory would rewrite the operator's own bases - the same accumulation that once put 198 stale records into the recovery-point registry."));
c.push(SP());
c.push(H2("4.4  Adding a vessel"));
c.push(P("Write `vessels/<id>.json` with every required field, start with `--vessel <id>`, and confirm the load message. There is no code to change. If a new subsystem needs a vessel-dependent value, add the field to the schema with a SENSIBLE DERIVED FALLBACK so that vessel files written before the field existed continue to load — see the ROC recovery standoff in chapter 9 for the pattern."));

// 5 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("5  Server reference (asv_console.py)"));
c.push(H2("5.1  Engine"));
c.push(P("`Engine` owns the link, the armed/e-stop state, the run state machine and the telemetry loop. It ticks the link, absorbs telemetry into a state snapshot, and pushes that snapshot to every SSE subscriber. It also resolves HOME on every tick (chapter 9) and drives the moving-HOME chase. It asks the link for each frame outside its lock, so a real link's read never delays a command - which means a command can land between a frame's read and its use. Every command that changes the run bumps a generation number, and a frame read before one is dropped rather than applied: applied, it put the run back the way it was, so a Stop read running and then complete, never stopped."));
c.push(P("THE TELEMETRY LOOP SURVIVES ITS OWN FAULTS, AND SAYS SO. An exception anywhere in a tick - a monitor fed a position, the state snapshot, the moving-HOME chase - used to end the loop's thread: telemetry stopped, and because the event stream keeps its connection alive with comments, the page's link dot stayed green over readouts that no longer moved. `_run` now restarts the loop on the next tick and records the fault on the state as `loop_fault` - the latest error, a count, and when the episode began. Each distinct raising line is printed with its traceback and logged once per episode - keyed on the line, not the message, because a message carrying a value differs on every tick - and the fault is taken down only after `LOOP_FAULT_CLEAR_TICKS` (8, two seconds) clean ticks in a row, so a fault that fires on every other tick stays one fault. A fault in the state snapshot itself cannot be published at all; the loop still survives it, and the page's stale check covers the silence. The handler's own reports are guarded as well: a write to a console window that has gone raises, and raised inside the handler it would end the loop after all. The state also carries `streaming`, true while a link exists - the loop publishes a frame on every tick then, healthy or lost - and that is what the page keys TELEMETRY STALE to. A fresh link starts with no fault."));
c.push(P("Run states are `idle`, `running`, `paused`, `stopped` and `complete`. Behaviors are `survey`, `goto`, `rth`, `transit`, `hold` and `escape`. Completion modes are `rth`, `complete`, `loiter` and `repeat`."));
c.push(H3("5.1.1  End-of-plan setting versus run completion"));
c.push(P("Two different things, deliberately kept in two different places. The END-OF-PLAN SETTING is the operator's persistent choice of what happens when a survey or search PLAN finishes; it is owned by the mission store and read through a cached accessor that every mission read and write refreshes. NO ENGINE METHOD WRITES IT. The RUN COMPLETION is what the run currently in progress does at its own end — a Go-To, return-to-home, hold or transit all station-keep at their endpoint, so a Go-To legitimately runs as “loiter”."));
c.push(P("They were one field once. Because a Go-To correctly set that field to loiter, commanding one silently overwrote the operator's end-of-plan selection: the command bar still showed the selection while the console acted on loiter, and the end-of-plan return-to-home chain, which gates on that value, was disarmed until the next plan start re-read the mission. Both values are now published on the state, the client reads the setting for the selector and the chain and the run value for the mode readout, and the selector mirrors the server's setting so the two cannot drift apart unnoticed."));
c.push(H3("5.1.2  ... and the END ACTION, which is what the operator asked"));
c.push(P("Neither field answers the question the cards are actually being read for: WHERE DOES THIS RUN LEAVE THE BOAT? The link holds at the last waypoint for both “loiter” and “rth”, and “rth” only becomes a return home because the CLIENT chains one on seeing that hold. So a Go-To or transit under End of Plan = RTH reported run completion “loiter” and every readout said the boat would stay at its endpoint — right up to the moment it turned for home. Each field was accurate about itself and wrong about the boat."));
c.push(P("`endAction()` in the client is the third value, and the only one the cards show. It is the run's own completion, upgraded to “rth” when the end-of-plan chain will fire, and downgraded from “rth” to “loiter” when it cannot: the chain needs a home, arming, no E-STOP, a run that actually ends (a repeat run laps forever and a complete run stops rather than holding), the LIVE setting rather than the frozen uploaded one, and a route home that was not refused. A card that promises a return the boat is not going to make is worse than one that never mentioned it."));
c.push(P("The chain is a one-shot per commanded run, and what re-arms it is the boat being UNDER WAY — not a run starting. The console lets a second Go-To be commanded while the first is still running, so the run state never leaves “running” and an idle-to-running edge never fires; that second run inherited a spent chain and held at its endpoint indefinitely. Invisible until the cards began naming the end state, at which point one sat there advertising a return home for five minutes."));
c.push(H2("5.2  SimVcu"));
c.push(P("The simulator: waypoint following by look-ahead line-of-sight guidance, a yaw-rate-capped heading response, a speed model, and a power model that is either battery voltage sag or diesel fuel burn depending on the vessel file. Wind and sea state from the environment monitor push it off track, and the guidance answers with a crab feedforward plus a cross-track integral term, the way a real autopilot does."));
c.push(H2("5.3  Supporting services"));
c.push(B("`CommsMonitor` — the radio link status readout."));
c.push(B("`WaterLevel` — real-time water level from public tide stations, ADDED to charted depths so the nogo model reflects the tide now, not the chart datum."));
c.push(P("THE MONITORS DO NOT FREEZE, AND THEIR READINGS CARRY AN AGE. `WaterLevel`, `EnvMonitor` and `CurrentsMonitor` each run their loop body through `_monitor_pass`. None of the three loops had a handler: one exception ended the thread and the reading froze, still marked ok and carrying no time - reproduced with an `http.client.IncompleteRead`, a connection cut mid-body, which `_coops_get` did not catch (it does now, and a station cache that is not JSON is fetched again). A pass that raises is recorded as `_error`, published as `monitor_error`, and printed when its text changes; the last good reading is kept and the next completed pass clears the error. Each snapshot carries `age_s`: the water level from the station's own observation time `t`, the weather from the oldest buoy observation in the blend (`obs_t`, read off the NDBC row), and the current from when it was computed. The current is a forecast for now, so it is recomputed from the cached cycle every `SAMPLE_S` (60 s) while the cycle itself is looked for every `POLL_S`, or at once on a move, a refresh or a model switch - and when no cycle can be had, the reading carries `_ensure_cycle`'s reason instead of \u201cno cycle cached yet\u201d, which used to overwrite it on the same pass. Each loop clears its wake-up flag only when a wait was actually woken by it: clearing after a wait that timed out swallowed a refresh, or a move, that landed between the two calls, until the next poll. The reason is kept from the last look through every sample until the next one; kept only on the pass that looked, the live card went back to \u201cno cycle cached yet\u201d a minute later."));
c.push(B("`EnvMonitor` — real wind and wave observations near the vessel, feeding the simulator's environmental forcing. Simulation only."));
c.push(P("WHEN NOAA'S MODEL SERVER IS DOWN, THE SAME FILES FROM ITS S3 BUCKET (9 October 2026). The model reader asks the CO-OPS THREDDS server for a subset of each forecast file; through 8 and 9 October that server answered HTTP 503 for every model. NOAA publishes the same netCDF-4 files to its open-data bucket on AWS, which answered throughout, but as whole files of 100-600 MB. ofs_s3.py reads them with the standard library: a reader for exactly the HDF5 structures those files were measured to use, which range-reads only the surface rows inside the box round the vessel - tens of kilobytes a frame, because the files are not compressed - and writes the reader's own cache, so the projection, the stream fusion and the Current pill work unchanged. The console tries it only when the server itself is down (a server error or no connection), and the Current tooltip says when a cycle came from there. It reads each file's own time units, which differ by model."));
c.push(P("NOAA STOFS-3D'S SURGE IN A PASSAGE'S WATER (13 October 2026; stofs.py, asv_console.py _stofs_lowered / surge_outlook / WaterLevel._surge_pass, asv.html sectionLevel / updateSurgeUI). Andy, of the SCHISM model's site: 'review this model for added utility ... Prioritize robustness and code reliability over speed', then 'approve 1, 1b, 2 and 6'. The forecast built on SCHISM is NOAA's Surge and Tide Operational Forecast System, STOFS-3D (Atlantic and Pacific), once a day at 12 UTC on NOAA's open-data bucket about six hours later; its classic-netCDF station file (about 16 MB) carries the combined water level (tide, surge and river, NAVD88) hourly at the tide gauges, 96 hours ahead and 24 before. A small reader checks every offset against the file and refuses anything else in words (CdfError); the module's own thread fetches the domain the boat is in (never on a Great Lake), keeps two cycles under charts/stofs, uses none older than 36 hours and wakes the water monitor when a new one is held - nothing in a request waits on a download, and an import starts nothing. Each station's series is TIED to the gauge by one constant from its observations over the hours both cover (refused past 0.30 m RMS, or 0.25 m from NOAA's datums) or, where the gauge has too few, by NOAA's NAVD88 offset (STOFS writes NAVD88: Lewes 0.81 m observed, NOAA's 0.801 m, its MSL 0.68 m). Its difference from the prediction - the SURGE - is AVERAGED OVER ONE TIDE (13 h): the model's tide runs minutes off the prediction's, which on Fort Point's rising tide read -0.28 m between +0.33 and +0.18 m an hour either side. fetch_water_along lowers each station's predictions by the negative part only (lower_by_surge: never above the prediction), every station asked in parallel within STOFS_ALONG_DEADLINE_S; a point it set carries src stofs and tide_low_m, and the answer a stofs block (cycle, age, used, notes); with no cycle held the answer is the one before, unchanged. The Mission Status card's SURGE row shows the surge at the nearest of her tide stations STOFS stands at, over 24 h. Measured on the 2026-10-13 cycle over the 12 hours it shared with the gauges' observations: STOFS 0.036 m RMS at Lewes where the predictions were 0.275 m off, 0.216 m at Portland against 0.351 m. A BORROWED SURGE (Andy approved it the same evening): the console's tide stations are NOAA's water-level list, which has Seavey Island near New Castle and not Fort Point, where STOFS's point is - so a station STOFS has no point at takes the surge of the nearest point within BORROW_KM (5 km; on that cycle 25 of the console's 168 tidal Atlantic stations had no point, 5 had one within 3.7 km and the next was 10.7 km), worked out at THAT point's gauge against its own prediction and tie (stofs.surge_borrowed: a surge is smooth over a few km, a tide is not, so two stations' tides are never compared). STOFS names a point by six digits of its gauge's id, so the gauge is found on NOAA's harmonic tide-prediction stations by number and place (asv_console.py _stofs_gauge / _tidepred_stations: cached under charts/, read again after 30 days, a failed read not tried again for 10 minutes); 160 of the 166 Atlantic points resolve. A station STOFS does stand at keeps its own answer, a refused fit never replaced by a neighbor's. The along answer's stofs block carries borrowed {station: gauge}; the card's tooltip names both, and its distance is to where the surge is."));
c.push(P("EACH MODEL'S OWN TIME ORIGIN (9 October 2026). The OFS models do not share one: read off each model's newest file that day, DBOFS, GOMOFS and WCOFS count seconds since 2016-01-01, LEOFS since 2015-01-01 and SSCOFS since 2018-01-01. The vendored currents.py took every raw time as seconds since 2016, so through NOAA's model server a Lake Erie cycle was cached a year late and a Salish Sea one two years early, and no frame ever covered now. Fixed in asv_core and vendored here: fetch_cycle reads the time units from each file's own attributes and converts them, and the file names stand as a second witness - a cycle whose frames land more than a day outside the hours its names give is refused in words naming both, never cached misdated. A model whose attributes cannot be read is taken as counting from 2016, as before, and the names refuse it if that was wrong. The cache still counts from 2016, so nothing that reads it changed."));
c.push(P("A MODEL THE STATIONS REJECT IS LEFT OUT NEAR THEM (9 October 2026). The calibration accepts a model at a station only at a correlation of at least 0.6 and a gain of 0.4 to 2.5, and until this change a model no station accepted was still blended in raw, at gain 1. Measured at New Castle with the console's own calibration on that day's GOMOFS cycle: the model has water at only 7 of the 26 prediction stations within 25 km, all at the harbor mouth - its 0.01-degree grid cannot resolve the river - and there it runs at about a tenth of the predicted strength, peaks of 0.09 to 0.33 kn against 0.97 to 2.17, in phase for about the first day of a cycle and out of phase later, so no window gives a gain the calibration accepts. Blended raw it had taken the port's reading from 0.64 kn to 0.47, and predicting each of those 7 stations from the others it made 3 worse, 3 slightly better and 1 no different. The same test on DBOFS at Lewes, the control, gave correlations of 0.88 to 1.00 and 15 of 16 stations accepting it. Now calibrate_all also records the stations that reject the model - it has water there and was fitted, and the fit fails - and where no station within 15 km fits the model and one there rejects it, fuse leaves the model out wherever a station reads, and the reading says why where the tooltip says 'No model in it'. Past the stations' 3 km reach the model is still the only layer and fills in as before; at that edge the old blend was already 95% model."));
c.push(B("`CurrentsMonitor` with `stream_fusion.StreamSources` — the surface current at any position, fused (2026-10-07). Its own thread holds NOAA's tidal current predictions for every station within 25 km (CO-OPS metadata and `currents_predictions`, the shallowest bin per location, weak-and-variable stations left out, a week ahead in `charts/coops_currents/`, refetched when a table reaches less than two days ahead) and, inside a PacIOOS domain, a one-day ERDDAP window of that model round the boat. The OFS the monitor already reads, or the PacIOOS window, is CALIBRATED to each station it has water at: the lag (within 90 minutes) and gain that best fit the model along the station's axis, kept only where the correlation is at least 0.6 and the gain between 0.4 and 2.5. `fuse()` answers at any (position, time): the stations by inverse distance squared out to 3 km, the calibrated model, and the two blended by exp(-d / 1 km) on the distance to the nearest station; None, never zero, where neither has a value. `field_at` is that answer for the simulator, asked every tick at her own position; `_sample` is the minute's reading, which carries its basis (`stations`, `model`, `w_stations`) and is field for field the OFS reading where no other layer reaches. A subordinate station's table is its events: between a slack and a strength it is interpolated as a quarter sine or cosine, between two strengths as a half cosine, and inside a 6-minute series linearly. Two switches: the environment switch (`POST /api/env {enabled:false}`, `EnvMonitor.is_enabled`) stops the simulator applying the stream as well as the wind and waves - the operations manual's deterministic calm run - while the readout still reports it; and `--no-stream-predictions` (`CurrentsMonitor.disable_sources`) reads the OFS alone, which every test harness's console is given (tests/lib/console_state.py), since thirty suites drive a console at New Castle where the stream is now the river's own. `stream_fusion.py` is in `BUILD_PY`, which the suites that start a console from a copy of the program now read rather than restate."));
c.push(B("`RocTracker` — Remote Operations Centers and HOME selection (chapter 9)."));
c.push(B("`SessionLogger` — append-only JSONL recording of every command, setting and telemetry sample."));

// 6 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("6  Client reference (static/asv.html)"));
c.push(P("One page, one module script scope - its lowest layer imported from `static/js/` - and no build step. It owns the slippy chart, the ENC feature set, all planning geometry, all route search, and every card."));
c.push(H2("6.1  Cards"));
c.push(P("THE PATH PLANNING CARD (13 October 2026; asv.html pathSelect / pathDraw / pathEscape / pathRender / pathReadings / setGotoTarget). Andy: 'Consider a single path planning card that holds all these behaviors and presents the necessary controls and readings for each when chosen. Kill the chips and permanently load the Path Planning card in the ASV Controls browser on start', then 'add Go-To and go', and 'Consider removing the repeats in the command bar'. The WPT / SURV / BND / SRCH / TRAN chips and their four cards became #pathPanel: five tabs over bodies that KEEP the old cards' ids (#linePanel - with the area, #boundPanel, inside it - #searchPanel, #transitPanel, and the new #gotoPanel and #wptPanel), because the controls window relays every click and edit BY ID. A TAB is what the card shows (pathTab, kept in asv_path_tab_v1); DRAWING is the chart's `mode`, and the two are apart on purpose: with the card always up, a tab that armed the chart would make every click meant to look at the chart a point in a plan. pathSelect arms the chart only for a tab with nothing drawn (pathEmpty: no pattern corner, no datum, no transit point, no Go-To point; Waypoints never - a click there ADDS a waypoint), stops another tab's drawing, and does nothing for the tab already showing; setMode brings a drawing mode's own tab (PATH_TAB_OF - the survey table's ✎ arms 'survey', the area 'bound' is the Survey tab's). Esc (pathEscape, after the ruler's own clauses), Add to plan (a committed pattern resets, a committed search drops its datum), Sail passage, Follow (doTransit) and Go end the drawing, and the chart says while it draws in its own pill (#r_draw), above the readout strip the card covers on a single window. The search preview is drawn while its TAB shows (searchShown), not only in its mode. The Go-To mode only PLACES the point (setGotoTarget; measure_tool 15d): the card's Go is doGoTo, gated on canCommand, as the chart menu's row always was. Readings: transit and search lengths, the transit time at the TRANSIT speed, the Go-To bearing, range and time, the plan at the foot - written only when they change, because the card is mirrored on every change. ROUTE RULES under every tab - Min depth, Water, Buffer, Transit kn, Channel rocks, the feature toggles - is the ONE copy: the command bar's Buf m, Min dep m and Transit kn went, so setBuffer, setMinDepth and setRoleSpeed write one control each. ONE bridged card, one uicard ('Path planning'), one resizable card (RESIZABLE_CARDS - the four old cards' .rsz squeezed the Survey body into a scroll box once nested). The first pathRender runs at the boot line: S, which canCommand reads, is declared far below the card's bindings."));
c.push(P("WAYPOINTS AND END OF PLAN (14 October 2026; asv.html #pp_follow, pathRender / pathReadings, syncCompletionSel). Andy: 'Move waypoint approach and arrival status and end of plan to the Path Planning card.' The command bar's Arr m, Appr m and End of plan are a section of the card under every tab, after Route rules, keeping their ids (#c_arrival, #c_approach, #c_completion) - so the mission load, the console's echo and the controls window's relay find them unchanged - folding like Route rules (PATH_FOLLOW_KEY) with a one-line summary. Moving the End of plan to a bridged card exposed a defect: syncCompletionSel puts the console's s.completion back on the selector every frame unless the selector has the focus, and the pick is saved 400 ms later (saveMission's debounce), so the frames in between carried the old value. Picked in the chart the selector kept the focus; picked in the controls window the chart's copy never has it - measured on a scratch console, Loiter picked there, the console stayed RTH and the controls window went back to RTH once its focus left. The pick is now held (completionAsked) until a frame reports it or COMPLETION_ECHO_MS (5 s) pass, after which the console's value is the truth (a refused save says so on its own)."));
c.push(P("THE MENU'S GO-TO FILLS THE GO-TO TAB (14 October 2026; Andy: 'When a GOTO is clicked on chart, fill in the latitude/longitude entries in the Path Planning Card'). The chart menu's Go-To row calls setGotoTarget(ll) - the fields, the point, its bearing and range, Go - and then returns doGoTo(ll), so cmRow still says when the command fails. The point is set BEFORE the command: a refused Go-To (measured on a scratch console: a point on land, 'Go-To refused: the target sits in land') leaves it in the tab for the operator to correct and send with Go."));
c.push(TBL(["Card", "Purpose"], [
  ["Path planning", "ONE card, always loaded, for every way a path is drawn (2026-10-13): Survey (the three-click pattern, its Area polygon, Punch Out, Add to plan), Search (canned patterns), Transit (Follow, or plan and sail a passage), Go-To and Waypoints tabs; DRAW arms the chart for the tab; ROUTE RULES under every tab; the plan at its foot"],
  ["Mission status", "Live telemetry, water level, nogo, comms — and the RUN block: one readout for every commanded run (type, waypoint progress, routed length, time to end, end mode, run time, status), shown only while there is a run to describe"],
  ["ROC · HOME", "Remote Operations Centers, recovery offsets, and HOME selection"],
  ["Chart source (`SRC`)", "Every ENC cell in view + the zone-of-confidence under the vessel"],
  ["Survey lines (`LINES`)", "Per-line planned versus actual run times - one row per DRAWN line, a line a keep-out cut read as its parts (`drawnLines` / `lineNo`)"],
  ["AIS", "Nearby traffic from the AIS service"],
], [2200, 7160]));
c.push(H2("6.2  Multi-monitor split"));
c.push(P("The console can run as two windows: a chart window and a controls window (`?panel=controls`), talking over a same-origin BroadcastChannel. The chart window is the single source of truth and owns all logic; the controls window renders no application logic, mirrors the control DOM, and forwards gestures back so every existing handler runs exactly once."));
c.push(P("CLOSED, NOT QUIET (2026-10-18; Andy: \"Stop moving cards to chart automatically unless the browser window closes. allow button to swap back and forth.\"). The chart used to count a controls page gone after UI_PEER_GONE_MS (6 s) without a word, and a controls window the browser slows, freezes or puts to sleep goes silent while open. Now each controls page takes a Web Lock of its own (`UI_LOCK` + its id) before its first hello and holds it for its life (a promise never settled; `uiLockFree` frees it only when the page steps aside, `uiYield`), and says so (`lock: true`) in every presence message. A chart that hears that waits on the same lock (`uiWatch`): the browser grants it only once the page has let go - closed, reloaded or crashed - and the grant drops the page (`peerBye`) and marks it closed (`uiClosed`), so a word still in flight does not bring it back; `uiPeersPrune` skips every page in `uiWatched`. A page that holds no lock - loaded before this, or a browser without Web Locks - still goes by its bye or its silence. Measured in the in-app browser on a scratch console: the controls tab's lock held and the chart's request pending while it was open; closed, the cards were back before the first look; silenced (its BroadcastChannel made to send nothing) for 12 s with the chart in view, the cards stayed. THE SWAP: `uiCardsHome` (\"controls\" or \"chart\"), owned by the supervising chart; `uiCardsTo(home)` sets it, re-applies the split (`uiSplitApply`: split = a controls window alive, this chart supervising, and the cards not on the chart) and sends the picture at once, which carries `home`; the controls window's `applyUISync` sets `ui-away` from it, hiding its toolbar and cards behind a note and a button. The chart's pill (#cardsSwap, shown under `ui-peer` - a controls window alive and this chart driving it - where #ctlReopen hides) and the controls window's own buttons (#cardsSwapCtl, #cardsHere: not mirrored, outside every bridged panel, so handled there, sending {t: \"home\"}) both call it; a view-only chart ignores it; a controls window arriving after none resets it to \"controls\" (peerSeen)."));
c.push(P("THE LISTEN QUEUE (2026-10-18). Everything is served no-store, so every load of the page asks for all its modules together; the stdlib's listen queue is 5, and Windows answers a connection a full queue cannot hold with a reset - ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED, no retry - where Linux lets it wait. Measured: a fresh Edge profile (nothing cached) opened the moment the console first answered had 21 of the page's module loads refused (1, another time; one is enough - the module graph fails), so the page never started and no Chart held the post; with `Server.request_queue_size` at 64, none, in six launches, the post held. A Python client's bursts of 29 at once were never refused, at start-up or after: only a browser's load in the console's first seconds was. Found while measuring Edge's sleeping tabs: a Chart tab behind another, sleeping tabs on at 5 minutes, stopped reporting at 5 minutes; the page cannot prevent that, and Edge has no per-tab switch, so the Controls window's red STOPPED ANSWERING banner ends with CHART_SLEEP_SAY - add 127.0.0.1 under \"Never put these sites to sleep\" (edge://settings/system/managePerformance)."));
c.push(P("The server opens those two and no more. The NOAA CO-OPS water-levels page for the station driving the depth correction is OFFERED by the page, not opened by the server (Andy, 2026-09-02: \"switch to one tab that re-points itself\"): `webbrowser` hands a URL to the operating system and gets no handle back, so a tab the server opened could never be re-pointed, only joined by a second one when the station moved. A page that opened a window holds its handle and may navigate it cross-origin, so the page owns the window: one click on the top bar's ⏏ Tide pill (`window.open` without a user gesture is blocked, measured), then `syncStationWindows()` re-points that same tab on every state poll whenever the station the server reports changes, and the window NAME re-adopts the tab across a reload of the console. The station id is DERIVED from the vessel fix, never configured — `tide_station_url()` is the single place the URL is built (the snapshot's `page`), and no station id appears as a literal anywhere in the console. With no fix there is no station and no page, so the pill waits on its own. `--no-tide-window` withdraws the pill (STATION_WINDOWS_ON, published as `station_windows` in the state), and `--browser none` opens no window at all, which is why the test harnesses are unaffected by it."));
c.push(P("The page takes ONE station id, so when the correction is an inverse-distance blend of up to WATER_K stations it shows the PRIMARY — the nearest one actually returning data, the same station the chart-source card attributes the correction to, both reading `water.station` so the two cannot disagree. Because a single station named against a blended number would misrepresent it, the console prints every contributor with its percentage share whenever the station changes (`watch_station_report()`, which opens nothing), and the card lists them in its Correction tooltip. Measured at the DriX spawn: Lewes 3.7 km at 95.5%, Brandywine Shoal Light 22.3 km at 2.6%, Cape May 26.4 km at 1.9% — inverse-SQUARE weighting means a near station dominates a far one heavily."));
c.push(P("The ⏏ Weather pill is the same mechanism pointed at the other network: the NDBC page for the weather buoy nearest the vessel. Both are entries in one server-side STATION_WINDOWS registry — each naming its snapshot source, its URL builder, its IDW power and its reach — read by a single watch_station_report() loop, and the page keeps a matching STATION_WINDOWS of its own (tide, weather and, since 2026-10-09, the current), so another page is another entry, not another copy that would only drift. Measured at the DriX spawn, the two networks resolve to the same three sites: LWSD1 3.7 km, BRND1 22.3 km, CMAN4 26.4 km, matching the CO-OPS trio because NDBC carries the co-located gauges."));
c.push(P("CONSEQUENCE FOR NEW UI: every interactive element needs a stable, unique `id`. The bridge forwards controls by id, so an id-less control is inert in the controls window. Handlers should key off class or `data-*` attributes so they stay id-agnostic."));
c.push(H2("6.2b  One supervising tab"));
c.push(P("The safety ladder is CLIENT-SIDE: `clearanceGuard`, `speedGovernor` and the end-of-plan RTH chain all run in the page. Two tabs are therefore two ladders commanding one boat, and a throttled or sleeping tab is a ladder that has stopped with nothing on screen to say so — and both look like ordinary traffic at the console. So each chart window carries a per-tab `CLIENT_ID`, POSTs `/api/supervisor` as it handles the event stream, and sends `X-ASV-Client` on every command; `Supervision` grants the post to one tab, publishes `supervisor` (with `beat_s` and `stale_s`, which the page adopts) in the state, and `supervisor_refusal` answers 409 to a command from a tab it KNOWS is not the holder. An anonymous caller (a script, a suite, curl) is deliberately not refused, and neither is `/api/mission`: the plan has its own revision guard, and a second screen may plan while the first supervises."));
c.push(P("In the page, `supervising()` gates three things and nothing else: `cmd()` (with STOP, PAUSE and E-STOP exempt in both directions), the two `act` flags in the guard and the governor — both AFTER the bar is drawn, so a view-only tab still assesses and alarms — and `canCommand`, which is what the chart menu's rows read. `applyViewOnly` sweeps the command bar by class rather than by a list of ids, so a control added later is gated by default. A holder that stops reporting goes `stale` after `SUPERVISOR_STALE_S` (10 s); a tab that has been reporting STEADILY — its previous report within `SUPERVISOR_STALE_S` — then takes the post on its next report, `pagehide` releases it immediately, and the console's own watch prints and logs the lapse once. The heartbeat is excluded from the session recording (`LOG_QUIET_POSTS`) while every supervision CHANGE is written as an event."));
c.push(P("WHAT WENT WRONG FIRST, AND WHY THE REPORT IS SHAPED THIS WAY (2026-09-16). As built, every page reported from a timer, and in Andy's session the post changed hands ten times in twelve minutes. Three causes, three rules. (1) The CONTROLS window reported in: it is the same page under `?panel=controls`, but it opens no stream, runs no ladder and forwards every click to the chart window — so while it held the post nothing ran the ladder (3 min 33 s at a stretch, armed) and the chart window refused the very clicks forwarded to it. `SUPERVISES` (`UIROLE === \"main\"`) now keeps it out: it never reports, never releases, and its `supervising()` is always false. (2) The report rode a TIMER, and the ladder rides the STREAM: measured on a hidden tab in headless Edge, 91 s in, one timer wake-up in 30 s against 121 frames handled — so a running ladder was called stale about once a minute. `supervisorReport()` now runs from `onFrame` after `onState` has run (a frame whose ladder threw is no report) and from the console's idle `tick` — a named event `_serve_events` sends when a beat passes with no frame, which `onmessage` never receives — at most once per `beat_s`; nothing reports from a timer. (3) A tab back from its OWN silence could take a stale post — two throttled windows woke once a minute and took it from each other — so a stale post goes only to a steady reporter. `SUPERVISOR_STALE_S` also went from 6 s to 10 s: every lapse in that session was 6.3-6.9 s old, one beat late, and a page carrying his 465-waypoint plan over 1,460 keep-out features was measured stalling 1.5-4.1 s at a time while idle."));
c.push(P("THE SLEEP RECORD. `supervisorStreamed()` runs at the top of every frame and tick and measures the gap since the last one; a gap over `stale_s` is `page_asleep` {gap_s, supervising, began_hidden}, and when it BEGAN off screen and the tab was supervising, the banner says the tab was asleep and for how long — whether or not the operator is looking by the time it wakes. A gap that began on screen is review #29's stall and is said there. `supervisorStreamLost()`, from the stream's `onerror`, drops the measurement, so a console restart is not called a sleep."));
c.push(P("A SEARCH IS NOT A STOP (2026-10-12). Andy's Go-To up the Piscataqua held the supervising page's thread 11 s in `planInsideChart`'s route search, read the chart wider, and held it 17.8 s more; the page could not report, the post went stale, his second chart tab (steady) took it each time, and the Go-To the planning tab then sent was refused 409. He saw it as TAKE OVER locking the tab: he had taken the post back during the wider read, and the second search started as the read finished. So before each search `planInsideChart` awaits `supervisorBusy()`, which POSTs `{id, busy_s: SUPERVISOR_BUSY_S}` (60) from the supervising chart window only, aborted after `SUPERVISOR_BUSY_WAIT_MS` (1.5 s) so a console that does not answer costs the plan at most that. `Supervision.beat` takes `busy_s` from the HOLDER alone (`_busy_seconds`: a finite positive number, else nothing), capped at `SUPERVISOR_BUSY_MAX_S` (60 s); until `_busy_until` the holder is not stale, so no steady tab takes the post; the holder's next ordinary report clears it, and TAKE OVER still moves the post and clears it. The snapshot carries `busy` (and `stale` is false while it holds) and `busy_max_s`; `check_once` says a plan that outlasts `stale_s` once, as a plan (`supervisor_busy`), and the lapse only once its time is out. The page stamps `supPlanEnd` after each search, and `supervisorSlept` records a gap ending within 2 s of one as `page_asleep` {planning: true} with no ASLEEP banner (the stall watchdog says an on-screen stall in its own words). A view-only tab's pill reads `the other tab is planning` while `busy`, and the supervising tab shows `⚠ N CHART TABS` while other chart tabs report. The search itself ran on the page's thread: the planner in a worker was the lasting fix, made the same day (6.2c), and this report now rides only the search that still does (planHere)."));
c.push(H2("6.2c  The route search in a worker"));
c.push(P("WHY (2026-10-12, Andy: \"move the route planner into a worker\"). A route search held the page's thread for as long as it ran, and with it the clearance guard, the readouts, the controls and supervision's report (6.2b). Every command's search - Go-To, RTH, a drawn transit, Upload, Continue, Resume from here and a passage's section - and the ETA rows' two now run in `static/js/route_worker.js`. route_job.js's `routeReply` gives the worker's own module instances the state the planner reads - `nogo.ko` (by id), `nogo.frame` (rebuilt from its `ref` by planeFrame), `nogo.buffer`, `bbox`, `unread`, `ready` and `ais`, and `V` - runs passage.js's own planNogoRoute, routePlan or holdTarget (`op` nogo / route / holdRoute: a drawn transit's or a section's hold point and its route, as one search), and hands back the answer with `sea.laneUsed` and `sea.lanePartial`. A field the planner comes to read that is not sent is a search on a different world from the page's, which is why tests/route_worker.js compares the worker's answer with the page's own, field for field."));
c.push(P("ON THE PAGE, `planOffThread(job, here, adopt, lane)` posts the search and awaits the answer; `here` is the same search on the page, which `planHere` runs (the busy report to the console first, 6.2b) whenever the worker cannot answer: no Worker, `?route_worker=off` on the page's address, a worker that would not load (given up for the page), a search it refused or threw on, or ROUTE_WORKER_TIMEOUT_MS (120 s: that worker is ended, and the next search makes a new one). The keep-out model is sent ONCE per model object: an id per object (`routeKoIds`, a WeakMap - rebuildNogo makes a new model rather than changing one), its feature count checked on every search (route_job.js koCount), and the page says what to keep (`keep`: the chart's own model), so a section's model at its own water (`koAtLevel`) or the way round a contact (`opts.ko`) goes with its search and is dropped after it. THE MODEL GOES PACKED AND TRANSFERRED (route_job.js packKo / unpackKo, 2026-10-12, Andy: \"go ahead with the transfer change\"): every ring and line point in one Float64Array whose buffer goes in postMessage's transfer list - handed over, the page's own array emptied by the post - and only the small records beside it cloned (deep water first's margin layer, `ko.margin`, the same way in the same array: 599 areas and 65,554 ring points on his 61 km Go-To's chart); the worker rebuilds each ring as {e, n} objects, exactly (a Float64Array holds each double as it is). Measured on the Piscataqua chart's model (2,033 keep-outs, 222,280 points): a structured clone of it cost the page 227-684 ms on top of the read's own work; packed, 17 ms to pack and 6 ms for the rest, and live the two posts of his Go-To took 17.6 and 37.4 ms with their buffers emptied, the worker's 306-waypoint answer identical to the page's own. The build itself is 67-94 ms on that chart, so building the model in the worker instead would have meant sending it BACK - the same copy - to a page that needs it on every frame. What the page still pauses for, about 1.1 s at each read on that chart (the meter's longest gaps, as each read landed), is the read's own work: taking in the 23.6 MB extract (its parse alone 248 ms), the seabed's gaps (skinGaps, 326 ms), the build and the redraw - not moved, since the page needs the features themselves (the punch, the chart image's read, the readouts) and copying them back measured 602 ms. Two workers, `cmd` and `est`, so an estimate never waits in front of a command; the estimates are not written to the session record. The guard's own searches - the deviation, the way round a contact, the re-approach and a held run's way back - stay on the page's thread: they are short, and they answer the frame they were asked on."));
c.push(P("A GRID OVER EVERY FEATURE LIST (2026-10-13, Andy: \"build the spatial index for the floor model\"; keepouts.js nearIn / fileList / gridOf). A point test - blocked, clearanceM and blockedInfo, and through them legClear, firstBlockAlong and every check the planner makes off the raster - walked every feature of the model, and on his 61 km Go-To's chart (1,634 areas, 1,509 lines, 1,510 points) that cost 43-85 us a test, nearly all of it bounding boxes nowhere near the point: `blocked` was 70 % of a long plan's CPU. Each feature list is now filed in GRID_CELL_M (250 m) cells - each feature, in the list's own order, in every cell its box grown by its own radius and GRID_PAD_M (100 m) touches. A test within GRID_PAD_M of a point reads that point's own cell, which holds every feature that could answer it; a wider one (a clearance capped at 500 m) reads every cell within its reach less the pad, each feature once; one reaching past GRID_WIDE_CELLS (64) cells walks the list. blockedInfo reads a wider set only by walking the list, so it names the feature the walk names. A list is filed once it holds GRID_MIN (16) features or more and has been asked GRID_AFTER (64) times - the planner's short-lived views of the model (the lane stages' walls, a contact folded in, one feature at a time) are walked as before - and a list that grows is filed afresh; the grids live in a WeakMap on the lists, and nothing writes on a feature. THE ANSWERS ARE THE WALK'S OWN: tests/keepout_index.js 8-14 hold blocked, the feature blockedInfo names and clearanceM to a nanometer against a plain re-walk of the exported primitives, on a chart of 3,100 features and on his charts about Oak Rock and the Dog Bar, at buffers and caps either side of the pad and past the widest reach, and the route the planner ships across Oak Rock clear by that walk. MEASURED on his 61 km Go-To's chart: a point test 2-6 us against 55-66 us walked (samples along his route at a 20 m clearance); the three grids built in 7 ms; the plan 4.1 s (17.4 s before) and 5.5 s with deep water first (23.2 s; each the mean of three runs alternating with the code before it), the routes byte-identical to those planned without the grid. Deep water first's margin layer reads through the same grid."));
c.push(P("MEASURED on a scratch console with his chart, his Go-To from New Castle up the Piscataqua on the DriX EM712: the first search 9.0 s in the worker (the route ran past the water read), the wider read, the second 24.1 s and 305 waypoints - the count his own Go-To shipped - with the page's longest pause in that minute 1.36 s and supervision never stale; and a second Go-To's answer (299 waypoints, from where she then was) identical, field for field, to planNogoRoute run on the page with the same arguments, state and model. On that two-core laptop the same search alternated three times, after a rest each, took 23.4 s in the worker against 20.1 s on the page: the page goes on working beside it."));
c.push(P("A STOP WITHDRAWS A PLAN STILL BEING SEARCHED. While the search held the thread, a Stop pressed during it waited behind it: the command posted first, and the Stop then stopped her. With the thread free the Stop goes at once, and the command posted when the search ended would start her again. So #b_stop, #b_pause (pausing, not resuming), #b_hold and #b_estop (latching, once confirmed) call `routeStopPressed`; planInsideChart notes `routeStopGen` as it begins and, after each search and at its end, answers `{withdrawn: true, error}` if it moved - said on the banner (\"Go-To withdrawn: STOP was pressed while its route was being planned. Nothing was sent.\") and recorded as `plan_withdrawn` - and each of the seven callers stands down on it (doRTH marks a chained RTH as failed; sailSend returns it as its error). Checked live: STOP pressed 2 s into a 26 s search stopped her at once, and the Go-To was withdrawn when the search ended. The console's own stops - the berth grant's, the guard's holds - withdraw nothing: they act on the route she is flying, and a new route is judged by the guard on its next frame. A Stop pressed during the chart read BEFORE planInsideChart begins (doGoTo's own ensureNogoCovers) is not caught, as before."));
c.push(H2("6.3  Telemetry health"));
c.push(P("Every telemetry frame runs the clearance guard, the speed governor, the re-approach and the end-of-plan chain inside `onState`, and the page used to catch and drop every error in it - a guard that had stopped running looked exactly like one with nothing to do. Frames now go through `onFrame`, and `consoleHealth` runs on every frame and once a second, so it follows each condition both ways. It says three things on the shared banner, and takes down only a banner of its own:"));
c.push(TBL(["Banner", "When", "Clears"], [
  ["TELEMETRY STALE", "No frame for `STALE_AFTER_MS` (2 s) while the last state said `streaming`. The link dot turns red and the pill beside it reads `stale`. Nothing about a fault is said then, because the page holds only the last frame.", "The next frame"],
  ["CONSOLE FAULT (this page)", "`FRAME_FAULT_AFTER` (3) frames failed in `onState`; the banner names the latest error, the first failure goes to the browser console, and the streak is logged once to the session log as `client:page_frame_fault`.", "`FRAME_CLEAR_AFTER` (8) clean frames in a row"],
  ["CONSOLE FAULT (the console)", "The state carries `loop_fault`: the count, the time it began and the latest error. Said after the page's own fault when both hold.", "When the console clears it"],
], [2200, 5160, 2000]));

c.push(H2("6.4  Page and program versions"));
c.push(P("The page and its modules are read from disk on every request, but the Python only when the console starts - so after a commit or an edit while a console ran, a refresh paired a new page with the old program: fields the page sent were dropped, routes it called answered 404, and nothing said why. `BuildWatch` fingerprints the console's own files - `BUILD_PY` (the console, `currents.py`, `roc_tracks.py`, `ais_service.py`, `gps_sim.py`), `static/*.html` and `static/js/*.js` - by content, with line endings normalized and names taken relative to the program's folder, so a copy elsewhere or a checkout that only rewrites line endings is the same version. It re-reads the files only when a size or modification time has changed, and looks no more often than every `BUILD_RECHECK_S` (5 s)."));
c.push(P("The state carries `build` (the fingerprint when the process started) and `build_on_disk` (now). The page is served with the fingerprint of the files as it was read, filled into `PAGE_BUILD`. `buildCheck`, on every state frame, shows the top-bar pill: RESTART CONSOLE when `build` differs from `build_on_disk`, or when the state has no `build` at all - a console from before this, which a page that knows to ask is newer than; RELOAD PAGE when a filled-in `PAGE_BUILD` differs from `build`; and nothing otherwise, including for a page that was not served by a console, whose `PAGE_BUILD` still holds the token."));

// 7 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("7  Behaviors, the nogo model and ENC-aware routing"));
c.push(P("Every behavior is chart-aware and arm-gated. Nothing moves the vessel until the operator arms."));
c.push(H2("7.1  The nogo model"));
c.push(P("Chart features are fetched from public electronic navigational chart services and reduced to keep-outs: shoreline, docks and piers, charted hazards, obstructions, and any water shallower than the vessel's derived minimum navigable depth. Charted depths are corrected by the live water level first. The result is a shared model that every behavior plans against, dilated by the vessel's nogo buffer."));
c.push(P("WHAT THE CHART SAYS, AND WHAT IT MEANS TO EACH HULL (2026-10-12, a review Andy asked for: every layer of every tile read, every chart component understood and applied by each vessel). Measured with the shipped code on his cached tiles, these reached the model as nothing, and are now applied (static/js/keepouts.js, asv_console.py ENC_ROLES): DREDGED AREAS and UNSURVEYED AREAS tile the seabed IN PLACE OF depth areas, and depthExcluded read depth areas alone - a dredged area is now judged by its DRVAL1 (the depth it is kept to) like a depth area, and enforced by the depth toggle even with the area toggle off; an unsurveyed area is under every floor. ISLETS charted as points or lines (land_point, land_line) are land under the land toggle, the point sized by the assumed extent (HAZ_SURFACE_SIZED) and never passed. OVERHEAD (bridge_span, overhead): a span, cable, pipeline or conveyor whose lower of VERCLR and VERCCL is under airNeedM - the hull's air_draft_m plus overhead_clearance_m (chart.js) - or which has none charted, is a keep-out (overheadBlocks); a hull with no air draft judges nothing overhead. Aquaculture, offshore platforms, fishing facilities, oil booms, light floats, the danger and cardinal beacons and dry docks have roles and kinds (CLS_KIND). hazPassable refuses a sounding whose QUASOU is 3, 4, 8 or 9 and a hazard whose WATLEV is 1, 2 or 7 (soundingUntrusted). READING: every band reads every layer it publishes (extras=True), the overview band is the last fill (ENC_BANDS) and the berthing band a whole supplement where its coverage reaches (ENC_BERTHING); a class resolves per band (Restricted_Area in the coastal, general and overview bands, Restricted_Area_area in harbour and approach; Pontoon_line in coastal); ENC_KEEP_PROPS keeps the clearances, WATLEV, QUASOU, EXPSOU, CATWRK, CATOBS, CATCAM, CATREA, INFORM, RESTRN, CONDTN, TRAFIC, CATBRG; a by-id answer that is an error payload or short of its ids fails the layer (_enc_query_by_ids, _one), a service refusal is retried (_enc_retry, ENC_QUERY_ATTEMPTS 3; a dead link is not); a tile missing a layer the model acts on (_ENC_ACTED_ON) is unread_failed water. CURRENCY: tiles are tile_v3 with read_at and reader; one past ENC_TILE_FRESH_S (7 days) is served and re-read behind (_tile_refresh_later); an older reader's copy is re-read first and served `stale` only when that read fails outright or misses a layer the model acts on (_fresh_falls_back; a fresh read missing only other layers is served), its bridge spans and overhead lines marked `clr_unread` - that reader kept no clearances - so overheadBlocks judges nothing on them (_clr_unread; 12 October: unmarked, every Piscataqua bridge was a wall to the DriX); anything not whole is held ENC_TILE_RETRY_S before it is read again; layer maps renew after 30 days; chart info is not kept after a failed query. Every query shares one TLS context (_enc_ssl): a context per request cost 22.5 s of a tile's 36 s of CPU. Roles are applied where a feature is served (_enc_retag), so a tile stored before a class had a role is served with today's. The passage grid (grid_v2) carries a third layer, A, the lowest overhead clearance per cell, judged against the hull's need by coarse.js tileLevels."));
c.push(P("EVERY CHART READ IS KEPT AND MERGED (2026-10-02, static/js/inkreads.js). The page held ONE chart-image read: a read of other water replaced it and a refused or failed one emptied it, so every structure found before left the model the planners and the clearance guard read. The budget is 256 tiles (CHART_INK_MAX_TILES): at 43 N about a 0.9 km square at z19 and 3.5 km at z17, the coarsest accepted; beyond it `inkTileGrid` refuses. An Upload read one box over the boat and every waypoint, so a spread plan read nothing and dropped the punches' piers. Now `chartReads` holds every read of the present extract ({key, bb, z, seq, lines, areas, detached}, at most INK_READS_MAX = 64, the oldest dropped), `refoldInk` merges them (`mergeReads`: finest first, at one zoom latest first; a line both of whose ends lie within INK_DUP_M = 15 m of a line already taken, or a footprint whose centroid lies inside one already taken, is the same structure) into `chartInk`, which keeps its shape, so the readout, `foldChartInk`, the drawn layer and the punch's memo key read it unchanged; the key names every read. NOTHING IS DROPPED FOR NOT BEING FOUND AGAIN: a coarse read's structure a finer read did not report is kept, because a tile the finer read never received is blank paper. `ensureChartInk` skips water a read already holds at the zoom it would get, or at INK_GOOD_Z = 18 (`coveringRead`); a refusal or a throw adds nothing and takes nothing away, and `inkRefused` puts 'the last water asked for was NOT read' on the readout until a read holds that water. A new extract still drops every read (`refreshNogo`). `readPlanInk` reads a plan's water: for an Upload (`ensureNogoCovers(..., {plan: true})`) the whole box if `inkTileGrid` accepts it, else the transits leg by leg - `pathThroughSurveys` (the boat, then each survey's first and last waypoint, free waypoints as they are) cut by `pathBoxes` into boxes that each fit, a box growing while it fits and the next opening at its last point, a leg too long halved, at most INK_PATH_MAX_BOXES = 16 with a banner for the rest - then each survey's own water (`surveyPoints`, padded INK_SURVEY_PAD_M = 100 m), which the punch's read usually already holds. Go-To, RTH, a transit and Set Home read their one box as before."));
c.push(P("THE SEABED'S OWN GAPS (2026-09-30). NOAA's vector service publishes no pontoon layer at harbour scale (only the coastal band lists one), so floats off a pier used to reach the model as nothing. At Pepperrell Cove survey lines and transits ran straight across them. The chart-image read missed them too: their outlines lay exactly on the depth areas' boundaries, which count as already charted. S-57's Group 1 - depth areas, dredged areas, land, floating docks, hulks, pontoons and unsurveyed areas - tiles the seabed with no gaps and no overlaps, so every pontoon is cut out of the depth areas around it, and those ARE served. On each extract the client (static/js/skin.js) orients every Group 1 ring with its polygon on the left. It cancels each edge that two polygons share; the edges match vertex for vertex - 92% of 183,639 on the New Castle extract, every vertex balanced. The leftover edges are chained into rings, and the clockwise ones are holes. A hole wholly inside the extract box becomes a dock-role feature with its own class and kind (one crossing the box may be filled by something beyond it, unfetched). As a feature, every model built from the extract has it: routing, the survey punch, the clearance guard and the chart-image read's explained mask and attachment pool. It follows the structures toggle and is outlined in orange on the NOGO layer. An extract missing a Group 1 layer is refused rather than read, since a missing depth layer would make the whole area one gap. Measured: New Castle 3 gaps (the two floats and one beside a pier at the shipyard), Lewes none, Los Angeles 7 along the San Pedro waterfront; the two Kittery floats and a Los Angeles one were checked against the chart image. About 60 ms warm and 280 ms cold on the main thread, in a turn of its own."));
c.push(P("A DASH THAT ENDS IN A DOT IS A LINE SYMBOL (2026-10-01). At Bellingham the chart draws a 1.4 km cable way southwest from the Whatcom Waterway as a 2 px stroke 31 px long with a 5 px dot on one end, repeated every 36 px at z18. NOAA's vector service carries no object along it, so its ink was unexplained, and CHAINING - which joins a pier drawn in pieces - joined 103 of the dashes into a 1,440 m mark that passed every shape gate. The proportional attachment allowance then let it count as attached 311 m off anything charted. `endDot` bins each piece's pixels along its own axis and reads a dot at either end: a compact blob, about as long as it is wide, at least two pixels and 1.75 times wider than the stroke's MEDIAN width. A T-head widens too but is not compact, and a pier head only slightly wider fails the ratio. `chainMarks` carries how many of a chain's pieces end in a dot, and `dashDotWhy` refuses a chain at least half of whose pieces (and at least two) do. It is refused as a dash-dot line symbol, never a footprint and never a detached report; a single dotted piece is left to the ordinary sieve. Over the live tiles: Bellingham 3 structures to 0, with 100 of 103, 8 of 10, 7 of 10 and 3 of 4 pieces dotted; the Kittery Point, New Castle and Fort Point piers byte-identical with the rule on and off (11 lines and 1 footprint, 10 and 2, 3 and 0)."));
c.push(P("What did NOT work, recorded so it is not tried again: painting only the outlines the chart actually draws into the image read's explained mask. Over the New Castle survey at z18 it added 5 lines and 13 false float-system footprints, up to 115 m. Those were the hachured drying lines round the ledges, which the same undrawn depth-area and zone-of-confidence rings had been explaining. Dropping only the magenta limits changed nothing. Every ring is still painted; the pontoons are read where they are exact."));
c.push(H3("7.1.1  Reporting the model's state"));
c.push(P("The vessel-status readout NAMES THE STATE rather than printing a count: reading the chart (with the elapsed seconds, so a chart service that has stopped answering does not look like a slow first fetch), a zone count with the vessel's own depth floor and a breakdown by keep-out kind, clear water, or the reason there is no model. The last two are the pair that matters. “The chart was read and there is nothing to avoid here” and “nothing has been checked, so every route is direct and unverified” both present as zero keep-outs and mean opposite things; only the second is a warning, and the readout must not let them look alike."));
c.push(P("Every state is derived from values the model already holds — whether an extract is in flight, whether a successful extract has ever landed, the reason recorded when one did not, and the keep-out counts — so there is no separate display state to drift out of step with what the behaviors route against."));
c.push(H2("7.2  Trusting the water level"));
c.push(P("Charted depths are corrected by the live water level before anything is classified as too shallow, so that correction is part of the safety model rather than a readout. A tide gauge reports the water level near ITS OWN station, and the console interpolates the nearest few \u2014 but if the nearest is hundreds of kilometers away, the number is another coast's tide."));
c.push(P("The reading is therefore banded by the distance to the nearest contributing station. Within a few tens of kilometers it is treated as local and applied normally. Further out it is applied but shown GHOSTED, because tidal range and phase drift materially over that distance. Beyond the remote threshold it is shown ghosted with an explicit warning and NOT APPLIED AT ALL: routing falls back to chart datum, exactly as it does when there is no data. Crediting the vessel with depth nobody has measured in that area is the same failure mode as trusting a charted symbol without its extent."));
c.push(P("The reading is also judged by its AGE. The monitor that keeps it could die and leave its last reading standing, still added to every charted depth, so the reading carries `age_s` from the station's own observation time. Measured across forty sessions (5,693 readings) a live level is 4.6 to 17.1 minutes old; past `WATER_STALE_S` (25 minutes) `waterTrust` marks it stale and `effectiveWaterOffset` does not apply it, whatever its distance band. A manual override is never stale, and a reading with no age at all - an older console - is not called stale: unknown is not old."));
c.push(P("The nearest station decides the banding, not the average of the contributors: an interpolation dominated by a close station is still local, and averaging would both discredit good readings and let two distant stations either side average into a falsely local one. An operator's manual override is always applied and never ghosted \u2014 it is their own number."));
c.push(H2("7.3  Charted hazards have an extent"));
c.push(P("A wreck symbol on a chart is a POSITION, not a size. The casualty beneath it can be a hundred meters long, and the electronic chart says nothing about its extent or which way it lies. Treating such a feature as a bare point and relying on the nogo buffer for clearance models a wrecked ship as a buffer-wide dot — a route then only has to miss the charted position by the buffer to validate clear."));
c.push(P("So hazards whose extent the chart does not give — wrecks, hulks, obstructions and awash rocks — carry an intrinsic radius, defaulting to 10 m and configurable per vessel, and the buffer is added ON TOP of it as the margin it was always meant to be. The default was 50 m until 9 October 2026, when it was lowered to 10 m for all four kinds, wrecks and hulks included: a casualty bigger than 10 m plus the buffer is therefore NOT covered by the default, and a vessel that wants more berth sets it in its own file. Objects that genuinely are point-sized, such as piles, buoys and beacons, are unaffected."));
c.push(P("Where the chart gives a sounding OVER the hazard, and that sounding — corrected to the live water level, exactly as depth areas are — clears the vessel's own navigability floor with margin, the vessel can pass over it and the hazard collapses back to a point. THE ABSENCE OF A SOUNDING MEANS UNKNOWN, and unknown takes the full berth rather than the benefit of the doubt."));
c.push(P("Both clearance paths honor this, and both must: the exact leg check that validates a planned path, AND the occupancy raster the route search runs over. If only the exact check knew about the extent, the search would plan straight through the hazard and the leg would simply fail — the vessel would get a refusal instead of a detour. A sized hazard also draws the circle the router keeps out of, so the operator can see why a route swings wide instead of reading a bare symbol as the whole danger."));
c.push(H2("7.4  Route search"));
c.push(B("Direct: an exact clearance check on the straight leg. If clear, done."));
c.push(B("`routeAroundSeg` — a fine sectioned search over a rasterized occupancy grid."));
c.push(B("Escalating swing regions when the detour leaves the search window — the grid coarsens, but the raster OVER-approximates keep-outs, so any coarse solution is genuinely clear and is then refined leg by leg."));
c.push(B("Fine-escape composition when a tight basin closes over the start or the goal: generate open-water escape candidates around the pinched end, route to one, then escalate from open water."));
c.push(P("The whole ladder runs under a time budget. An exhausted budget or a genuine absence of any clear path is reported as unroutable and refused, with the obstruction named. The console never plans a leg it has not verified."));
c.push(P("THE TRANSIT STANDOFF (Andy, 2026-10-06, after his two holds at Little Bay: 'if the ASV is near a danger, it should open the distance from said danger by workable distances within constraints toward deeper water' - and, of the numbers offered, 'use the turn radius at the plan speed'). A transit - a Go-To, a Return-to-Home, a drawn transit line, the Upload's approach and the leg into each later survey - is searched at `standoffM` (planNogoRoute, routePlan), and that number is now guard.js `transitStandoffM(buffer, set, speed, turnRate)`: the guard's own standoff (`guardStandoffM`, the helm rung's immunity in the set, floored at the buffer) or the hull's turn radius at the speed the plan will be flown (v / omega at `maneuvering.max_turn_rate_deg_s`, the radius the look-ahead integrates and the turns are built on), whichever is the larger. The page's `transitStandoffM(role)` hands it the transit role's speed and the hull's rate and does no arithmetic of its own, the same discipline as `patClipBufM` (planner_guard_seam 9b). The DriX at 20 degrees a second: 5.9 m at 4 kn, 10.3 at 7, 20.6 at 14 - where a transit in calm water used to be laid at the bare buffer (his 10:32:20 Go-To at 13.7 kn passed a charted rock's 50 m disc 5.9 m off in a river 400 m wide). The fallback is unchanged: where the water will not allow the standoff the leg is found at the buffer, counted (`insideStandoff`) and said on the banner. The coverage lines are not transits and keep `patClipBufM` (coverage is the operator's; a line's turns are built on the radius already); the re-approach onto station (the hold speed) and the way round a contact (its own ring, tuned on the water) keep it too. 'Toward deeper water' among navigable water is not modeled: the standoff opens the distance from the shallow and the hard; a depth-weighted search is a later option. Pinned by planner_guard_seam 9-9c."));
c.push(P("THE PASSAGE TAB, ENDS FIRST, AND A LONG GO-TO AS A PASSAGE (14 October 2026; asv.html passageWps / passageEdited / goToOrPassage / planPassageEnds / planPassageEnd / passageEndWorld / passageHalfKm / drawPassageWps, planOffThread's `world`). Andy: 'consider another path planning behavior called passage that disregards the 9-23 directive and allows for long path planning'; 'do it in segments starting with beginning and end and then working inward'; and, asked, a Go-To past 50 km 'Always a passage' on a diesel hull, a battery hull's a box. The card's sixth tab owns its own line (P1..., the last the arrival, a point clicked again a fuel stop) and the three passage buttons, moved from the Transit tab with their ids (the controls window relays by id); a transit edit no longer drops a passage. The corridor read along it reaches passageHalfKm either side - a fifth of the longest leg, 5 to 25 km. Once the coarse plan is in, planPassageEnds plans its first and last sections in detail: passageEndWorld reads the section's box (PASSAGE_END_PAD_M round it) straight from /api/enc - not through fetchENCBbox, which writes the page's sea.enc - and builds a model and margin layer of its own, and planOffThread sends that world (its chart box, unread squares, frame, no AIS contacts) to the route worker with the section's holdRoute job; with no worker it answers nothing rather than plan on the page's thread, which holds the page's chart. sailRefusal refuses a plan whose ends were not planned (passageEndsSay names 'leaving' / 'arriving'). goToOrPassage is the operator's Go-To (the chart menu's row, the Go-To tab's Go): past RTH_MAX_M on a hull passageRefusal allows, armed, it sets the line to the point, plans, and sails unless sailRefusal; every other Go-To is doGoTo's box. Live: his 118 km Portland -> Penobscot Go-To on the DriX read 27 tiles, planned 7 sections, both ends planned (leaving 20.5 km, arriving 7.9 km), section 1 sent."));
c.push(P("THE BOX CAP AND THE DRAWING (14 October 2026; asv_console.py and asv.html ENC_MAX_SPAN_DEG 2.0; featureBox / layerSees / tracePx, in static/js/chartlayers.js since 17 October). 1.5 degrees refused open water's wider read on his 120 km Go-To (1.71 x 0.73). The cost of a wider box, measured on his session: 21 tiles of about 11 MB read in 65 s, 14,534 zones, zoom changes stalling the page 1.6 to 12.9 s - every feature of the box projected and traced on each, on or off the canvas. drawENC and drawNogo now skip a feature whose box is off the layer's canvas, trace no vertex within 0.7 px of the last (a ring's last always), and draw no area or line under half a pixel (a hazard's symbol whatever its size): on 22,661 zones each zoom change took 0.32 to 1.07 s, against 0.73 to 3.22 s on 20,078 before. The picture only; the model is untouched. Building a model that size still holds the page several seconds."));
c.push(P("THE LAYERS DRAWN OFF THE PAGE, AND THE ROUTE GRID BUILT FASTER (17 October 2026; static/js/chartlayers.js, static/js/skin_worker.js, asv.html drawLayerOff / layerAnswer / layerStale; static/js/raster.js rasterKeepouts / dilateGrid). Items 4 and 5 of the performance review. The ENC, keep-out and shore-band layers are drawn by one module that the page and the chart worker both import. The worker keeps the extract it parsed for the seabed walk (the two used most recently), draws a layer when the page asks - the view and its margin, the extract's id, and the state the drawing reads - and sends the bitmap back; the page shows the last picture, moved, or scaled across a zoom, until it lands. One request a layer at a time. The page draws a layer itself, as before, for an extract the worker does not hold, a failed answer or no answer in LAYER_WORKER_TIMEOUT_MS (15 s), and for that extract from then on. At New Castle in an 1100 x 800 view a zoom held the page for one frame of 96 to 197 ms before, and for 10 ms at most after. The route search's grid is built in about half the time and is the same grid, cell for cell (tests/raster_exact.js). A GPU grid was weighed and not built: WebGL's rasterizer cannot be made to draw the same cells, and this grid must never under-draw a keep-out; it could not be tested outside a browser; and it could save at most the 0.2 to 0.7 s the grid now takes a search."));
c.push(P("THE ROUTE STAYS INSIDE THE BOX THE CHART WAS READ OVER (2026-10-03; beyondChart in static/js/passage.js, planInsideChart in static/asv.html). The model holds the features of one box, nogo.bbox, and outside it nothing, which every clearance test reads as clear. A command reads the chart over its own points padded 300 m (ensureNogoCovers), and the swing regions above reach 900 m to 8.1 km past the leg. planNogoRoute judges the route AS FLOWN - from the start, after the lane pass - and refuses it (mode uncharted, and no route a caller could fly) when any point stands within CHART_EDGE_M (100 m) of the box edge; routePlan returns it with uncharted beside it. A box is convex, so every leg of a route whose points are all inside is inside. planInsideChart then reads the chart over the command's points and the refused route (padded CHART_WIDEN_PAD_M, 1 km: with 300 m a replan that drifted into the margin needed a second read every time the review's fuzz saw one), reads the command's own water again with its own options, takes the AIS contacts again in the new frame (rebuildNogo re-centers the frame on the features read), and plans again: at most CHART_WIDEN_ROUNDS (3) reads. Every read it asks for is a keep read (refreshNogo's ask.keep): one that fails - no band, a throw, an extract missing a layer - leaves the model as it was and says why in ask.failed, where it used to set nogo.ready false and stand the guard down. A wider extract of the same chart (it contains the last box, at the same band and floor) keeps the chart-image reads. The hold disc a plan ships is capped at the hold point's distance to the box edge, less the buffer (discInChart). resumeFromHere plans its way in through the loop, with her and the contacts' model taken again inside the plan; tautRoundHer hands a route past the box to the router. planInsideChart refuses without planning when the command's own points cannot be read - planning to a target 1.5 to 2 degrees off over an empty model was measured at 15 to 31 s of the page's only thread. refreshNogo never asks the server for a box wider than ENC_MAX_SPAN_DEG, the server's own limit: its 400 used to set nogo.ready false, and the next plan went out degraded, straight and unchecked. The AIS way-round, the held resumes and the re-approach plan with planNogoRoute alone and get its refusal (planWhy says it in the operator's units), not the reads."));
c.push(P("THE VESSELS NOT UNDER WAY ARE IN A PASSAGE'S WATER (2026-10-07; aisMooredKeepouts in static/js/ais_keepout.js, passageKo and mooredPassed in static/js/passage.js, readPassageAis in static/asv.html). Before a Go-To, an RTH, a drawn transit or an Upload is planned, planInsideChart reads the AIS proxy along the plan's own legs - the proxy answers within its display radius of one center, so from centers 1.5 radii apart, at most PASSAGE_AIS_MAX_READS (8), each bounded by PASSAGE_AIS_TIMEOUT_MS, merged by MMSI; one read on a Great Lake - into nogo.ais. passageKo then lays the passage in a COPY of the charted model with every vessel aisMotion calls NOT under way (stopped, moored, at anchor, or reporting like one) folded in as the guard models her - her hull, unswept, anywhere in the chart box rather than within AIS_KO_RANGE_M of the boat - and the router keeps its standoff outside her as outside a pier, the target held off her as off one. nogo.ko itself is never touched: the punch and the readouts are built from it, and the guard models every contact itself. A vessel under way is never in it; a read older than AIS_KO_STALE_S, or none (the proxy down), lays nothing and the passage is planned as it was. In routePlan only the transit legs - the approach, a pure transit, the leg into each later survey - get it; a pattern's own lines and hops are flown as drawn. The plan names the vessels it passes within MOORED_SAY_M (250 m), and the Go-To, RTH and Transit banners say them (\"clear of NORD LOGOS (132 x 22 m, moored) (AIS)\"). Found on his RTH outbound past the Newington pier: 79 m off a moored 132 m ship's fix at 10 kn, which neither the route nor the guard - it answers a projected ENTRY into her hull - had said a word about."));
c.push(H2("7.5  The channel lane (COLREGS Rule 9)"));
c.push(P("In a narrow channel or fairway the vessel keeps to the starboard side. The lane is a purely geometric rule: offset to starboard of the channel centerline, half way out to the edge on that side — a quarter of the full width in from the edge. For a lane, buoy COLOR is never an input; it falls out, because lateral marks sit on fixed sides. Opposing traffic therefore passes port to port."));
c.push(P("THE CHART FIRST, THEN THE BUOYS (2026-10-04; channelLaneRoute in static/js/routing.js). The operator: a channel on the ENC is a charted Fairway_area or Dredged_Area at any width, recognized before any buoy; buoys are secondary to it, and primary without it; red right returning, singles included; a buoy kept to starboard passed at max(10 m, 2 x buffer). The pipeline runs the buoy-pair lane and the banks' lane as before, then the single marks (markPassRoute) in water the chart does not own, then the CHART'S lane (chartedChannelLane), which overrides only the samples chartOwnership gives the chart - a mark's run included, where it brings her into the channel - and leaves every other vertex exactly where it was, then the buoyed reach between single marks where the chart draws none (buoyedReachLane, below), then smoothTrack, gateLegClear and pruneStitch, with the marks' runs protected from the smoothing and from the chart's lane outside the water it owns, and their pass points from the knot prune (a run gives way at its ends)."));
c.push(P("Which water the chart owns, and where on it (chartOwnership). The path is resampled every max(25, 7 x buffer) m. A sample is the chart's where she proceeds ALONG a charted channel - the polygon runs on along her course at least 1.5 times its width and at least a real stretch (max(120, 30 x buffer) m) - in a stretch that long that she is actually inside somewhere; a path beside the channel is captured within LANE_CAPTURE_STANDOFF_M, and a bend cut between two stretches of it within CHART_BRIDGE_REACH_M - its bounding measured samples found past up to CHART_TAN_M of charted water the measurement declined at the edge she crosses, and each of its samples asked only for a cross-section within that reach over open water, not the along test, which no bend cut passes (2026-10-07: outbound past the Newington pier a cut stepping out over ONE declined sample was never bridged, and 525 m of route left the channel past a 132 m ship moored there - only at a standoff over 21.5 m). The cross-section is the POLYGON's, square to her course over CHART_TAN_M (75 m) either way; on the fairway's own rings wherever she is in a fairway, and wherever she stands in a dredged area whose cross-section runs on, in charted water, into one. A side standing further out than that side's median over a 600 m window - read in the sample's own frame from neighbors running within 10 degrees of its course - by max(30 m, 0.15 x width) is an OPENING, and its edges are carried across from good samples (CHART_INTERP_M, gaps up to CHART_GAP_M), clipped from inside the carried channel by the polygon's own edge. The lane is three quarters across, the nearest clear point to it where that is foul, never to port of the middle; capped by a cone at LANE_SLEW read in each sample's OWN FRAME - a sample's band the nearest of its own limits (its chord's edges, standoff in, across its own track) and of every SETTLED owned neighbor's within CHART_CONE_REACH_M (600 m) - her path running straight through it (CHART_CONE_STRAIGHT_COS, 3 degrees) and its cross-section holding her - each as that neighbor measured it across its own track, round a bend too: the offsets chartedChannelLane eases the lane in (slewField). So it is inside a narrower stretch ahead or behind by the time she reaches it, and a narrowing just round a bend is eased toward before the bend. The middle yields only to the stretch she runs straight along: a neighbor there may take the lane to port of the sample's own chord's middle (the narrowing is real, and staying in the charted water needs it); any other neighbor's eases it to that middle and no further (INVARIANT 4), so a routed path that jogs off the channel cannot take the lane to port (beside a jog it can hold it toward the middle). Clamped at the middle everywhere, a narrow part whose starboard edge stepped in past the wide part's middle was reached 8-33 m out of the charted water; reading the cross-sections where her path still turns, at the join of two charted rings at a 40 degree port bend - each cut short by the end of the ring she leaves - the route ran 23-42 m to port of both channels' middles. Read from the neighbors running its own way alone (within 3 degrees), a width step round a bend of 5 to 45 degrees carried the lane 12-25 m out of the charted water past the narrow part's starboard edge; measured across the search's path, a jog of that path moved the band; projected into one plane, a winding fairway was laned left of its middle. Where the channel ENDS ahead of her lane within the water she needs to ease back in, the samples beyond it carry the lane on (ramps); through its side she eases off inside it (slewField's pins). A ramp carries a lane to STARBOARD of her path only (2026-10-10): a lane to port of it - her path outside the channel's starboard edge - is a move Rule 9 asks for only inside the channel, and carried past the end as an offset there is no channel to say where it puts her. Bound up the Piscataqua the fairway's line was 49 m to port of a path running 21 m outside it at the Memorial Bridge, and carried six samples back along a path bending south below the bridge it took her through the south half of the lift span and along the south side of the reach below it; such a lane eases in inside the channel (tests/channel_end.js). An end is her lane leaving that end's OWN ring, whatever charted water lies beyond it (a fairway into a dredged basin ends there), and the ramp is laid only where her PATH leaves the water the run ends in - the rings her lane and her path stand in there - before her route ends: where her route ends in the channel she leaves the lane inside it (at Pearl Harbor the lane was carried to the end and hooked back round to her destination). A run's end samples within CHART_TAN_M of its end whose cross-section is narrower than CHART_END_SLIVER of the run's median - and is NOT so narrow measured square to the run's own direction (`across`) - are the END's: a turn clips its corner. They are not owned where her path goes on out of it; a channel that really narrows at its end is the channel, and is laned to its end. Where the cone's band inverts - the neighbors' own limits moving across her path faster than LANE_SLEW, as at the join of two charted rings - a sample keeps its own lane point, so every owned lane point stands inside its own cross-section (the middle of an inverted band put it 114 m, and at a 19.5 m standoff 1,048 m, outside the channel). In a set (opts.standoffM) the lane point is the nearest clear of the guard's standoff right of the middle, and failing that the nearest clear of the buffer, as before."));
c.push(P("The buoyed reach (buoyedReachLane; Andy, 2026-10-04, of the Piscataqua past Seavey Island: 'the ASV moves to the far side of the channel both outbound and inbound'). markPassRoute leaves each mark on its proper hand and no more: a mark kept to port is only given a berth, and a beacon on the starboard hand is never brought close - so where a reach's starboard-hand marks are beacons on the shore, nothing put her on the starboard side of the water the marks buoy. A reach is the stretch of her route between two marks no more than MARK_NEIGHBOR_M apart along it, the hand of at least one of them read (the other may be the chart's or the pair lane's). Its edges, each side of her: the bank (keep-outs at her margin other than the rocks, STANDOFF in, as far as REACH_EDGE_M, 600 m; an opening read across - where the water's WIDTH, the two banks' distances together, exceeds its median along CHART_EDGE_WINDOW_M either way by more than the greater of CHART_OPENING_M and CHART_OPENING_FRAC of it, the excess is side water and is taken off the side that stands further off than its own median. The width and not either bank's distance: the banks are measured from her path, and her path is the search's, so crossing a river on the chord of a bend, or swung round a buoy in mid-water, one bank draws off as the other closes and the water is no wider - judged a side at a time that was an opening on the outside of every bend she cut); a LOCAL charted restricted area wherever she is outside it (ko.restricted, area at most RESTRICTED_LOCAL_M2, 1 km2, judged over the whole of a named area the chart cuts at its cell seams - 11 pieces of a 210 km2 whale habitat each passed as local - the chart layer also holds a no-discharge zone covering half the river, and the service publishes no attribute that tells the two apart; excused WHOLE where one sample of her path stood in it, an area with a spur reaching 1 m past her path let the lane ride 1,460 m through the rest of it - so it bounds every sample outside it, and a sample inside it is moved only where its nearer edge is to starboard, `deeper`); and that hand's marks - the line from one to the next where the WATER's course between them turns no more than 10 degrees (REACH_STRAIGHT_COS), and in the stretch from one mark of either hand to the next the line through the mark at either end of it along the water's course (REACH_RUN_COS, 20 degrees), each measured in the plane across the sample's own cross-section. The water's course is the way the middle of the water between her banks runs over MARK_COURSE_LOOK_M either way - her own course only where she has no two banks - because her course is the search's, the shortest way, and between two buoys on the outside of a bend it is the straight line that cuts the bend off. She rides three quarters across (LANE_FRAC), only ever moved to starboard, eased at LANE_SLEW - over a stretch of cross-sections at least REACH_MIN_M (150 m) long, laned no further off her path than that stretch is long at LANE_SLEW; and a cross-section is asked of the edges as they TYPICALLY stand over the window, so an obstruction in water that is no channel (a rock in a river a kilometer wide, an islet off an open shore - the rock once made ONE sample a cross-section and swung 575 m of route 90 m toward it) is something to stay off, not a channel's edge. A port-hand mark that stands to starboard of her gives no cross-section, as a starboard-hand mark to port of her never has: she is on its wrong side, which is the marks stage's to say (laned, a green buoy 40 m to starboard of her was steered at and passed 5.6 m off). THE CONE caps the lane at every starboard limit ahead and astern, eased at the same slope: at EVERY lateral mark on her starboard hand, hand read or not (a buoy kept to starboard its pass distance in; any other mark - a beacon, an unnumbered or junction buoy, a port-hand mark she is on the wrong side of - a beacon's berth: left out of the cone, an unnumbered red buoy 140 m to starboard of her was shipped 42 m to PORT), by the water between any sample and such a mark, and to nothing at the pass points of a run past a mark kept to starboard (the run's ends give way). Each limit read at one sample - a bank, a mark - holds over a smoothing step either side of it (KH samples): a notch one sample wide was a V whose point the smoothing cut off, and 72c's pier, which the search cleared by 60 m, shipped 3.5 m off its head. It runs AFTER the chart's own lane, on the path that lane left, and in the water that lane rides moves her no further than that lane's own line (`owns`, the slack): the chart's lane is handed exactly the path it always was. A short stretch with no cross-section between two that have one is held across from either side, as the lane is held past a reach's end. And it is kept only where the route that SHIPS is no worse for it (channelLaneRoute's `finish`: the smoothing, the gate and the knot prune run on the lane's path and on the path without it, and the route without it ships where the lane's has a mark on its wrong hand that it has right - mark by mark, not by the count - or was abandoned where it was not, or has a mark NO count speaks for (no number, a junction mark) between the two routes - a counted mark the lane puts right lies between them by definition, and asked of every mark that vetoed every such correction - or a turn over MARK_JOIN_MAX_DEG that it has nothing as sharp within NEW_TURN_NEAR_M of (newLaneTurn: 35 degrees laid at a pass point shipped as 54 with the lane's ease beside it, and compared as whole-route maxima a 57 degree S-turn beside Seavey Island shipped because the route without the lane turned 75 degrees 3.4 km away), or a keep-out passed nearer than its floor - the lane's own, clr + STANDOFF, or a rock's, its model's (below) - and by more than the buffer or 3 m, the less, nearer than the route without it (`grazes`: the smoothing cuts the V the lane makes round what stands off her path, and a rock 15 m off her path was passed 6.2 m off). A GRAZE IS ANSWERED WHERE IT IS (Andy, 2026-10-08, of his RTH past Pierce Island: 'the ASV shies away from Pierce and Goat islands and violates the stay-right protocol'): a lane that fails only that last test is laid again HELD OFF what it grazed (buoyedReachLane `hold`: at every sample a shift could bring within the hazard's floor she is not moved, and the cone eases the lane down to that and back up from it at LANE_SLEW) and judged again, up to REACH_HOLD_ROUNDS (3) times - a round with nothing new to hold ends them - and is dropped for the whole route only if it still grazes or fails any other test, which is never answered locally. Dropped whole at its first graze, the lane was lost down all of his 09:19 RTH for one dock at the Memorial Bridge, which it passed 24.2 m off where the route without it passed 29.7 m (the floor 33.8 m, from his 2.36 kn set's standoff) - keep-right lost past Pierce Island and Henderson Point 1-2 km on, and in Little Bay 12 km back up the river; held off the dock, the lane stands everywhere else. Not measured sample by sample: a graze the smoothing makes is not seen at the samples (the lane 280 m to starboard of her path dipped back inside a bridge support 15 m off it and the smoothing cut the dip's corner to 6.2 m off it, every sample of the dip 9 m or more off). In a set the planner re-gates that route at the standoff (passage.js keepStandoff), whose splices have no notion of a side; the route without the lane goes back with it (`withoutReach`) and is re-gated too, and the route without the lane ships there where the laned one has a mark on its wrong hand that it has right, mark by mark, or was abandoned where it was not - the crossing, the turn and the clearance tests are not asked again (a green buoy both routes had right at the buffer came back 15.5 m on her wrong hand with the lane). In a set its points are laid clear of the guard's standoff; and a mark kept to starboard that has no run of its own and stands no further off her path than its pass distance and a march step has nothing moved within two smoothing steps (2 x HOLD) of it either side. Nothing of the bank stands between her path and her lane: at any sample she is moved no further than the water on her starboard hand there (`room`: the bank as the reach's own samples read it, STANDOFF in), and past a reach's end - where the lane is held while it eases off, over samples that have no cross-section of their own - the hold stops at the first sample, or a smoothing step either side of it, whose water is not open as far as the ease leaves her there. Asked only whether the lane's own point was clear, it was laid 135 m to starboard of her path short of Hen Island Ledge Buoy 1 with a charted islet 70 m off that path between the two, and she was taken round the far side of the islet (since the rock models, an islet with water all round it is a rock, passed either side - below; a pier's head or a point of the shore still stops the hold). And a bank is read BETWEEN the rays as well as along them: a ray every STEP sees only its own margin, and an islet 14 m long, a charted line, a rock with no extent or a small restricted area between two rays was unseen and passed on its far side - so each corner of a keep-out, each charted hazard (less its own extent) and each corner of a local restricted area on her starboard hand is read at the samples either side of it (`obs`) - the bank's, not a rock's (below). Water wider than REACH_MAX_WIDTH_M (800 m) between its edges is no narrow channel - asked of the cross-section itself as well as of its edges as they typically stand: where the water from a reach's line of green buoys to the far bank's standoff, 781 m, widens to 881 m for 400 m - too little to be an opening - she holds the lane she rides either side of it (asked of the typical edges alone she was taken 67 m further out across it, and on a real search path up the Piscataqua at a 5 m buffer two cross-sections 815 and 835 m across moved the route that shipped 22 m across the river) - and with no marks two banks 400 m apart still move nothing."));
c.push(P("WHERE ITS CROSS-SECTIONS FALL (2026-10-10; resampleAnchoredEN). The reach lane reads her route every STEP, and the samples are ANCHORED at the reach's marks: the grid starts again at each mark at least REACH_ANCHOR_GAP (6) steps past the one before it, or past her start, and short of her end, evenly spaced between two anchors - within a twelfth of a step. Begun at her route's first point, every sample fell where the route's length upstream put it, and the lane's caps, read at the apex of a turn and eased from there at LANE_SLEW a sample, moved with it: on his westbound Go-To up the Piscataqua a route 10.9 m longer below the Memorial Bridge moved the lane at Fox Point 27 m, and over one sample period the lane on that route swung up to 68.5 m. Anchored, it moves 0.000 m past the first anchor. Whether a mark anchors is decided by the marks' own spacing, never by where her route begins. (Every other lane still reads from her route's first point.)"));
c.push(P("A ROCK IS A SHALLOW POINT, NOT AN EDGE (Andy, 2026-10-05, on the review: 'hold the lane and pass the rock wide. do not use a rock as an assumed buoy for path planning purposes. treat it as land that may be avoided to either side' - 'maybe treat a rock as sea bottom - a shallow point to be avoided' - 'model either way for testing purposes'). Read as a bank, a charted rock 130 m off her path in a 500 m buoyed river pulled the lane in from 276 m to 112 m, to pass it 18 m off on the side her path had passed it; held, the lane passes it 146 m off. A ROCK (buoyedReachLane) is a keep-out of a natural kind - a charted hazard, land, the shoreline, water shallower than the floor (`natural`) - whose chart class, where it carries one (keepouts.js carries each keep-out's `cls`), is in ROCK_CLASSES: a rock awash or under water (Underwater_Awash_Rock_point), an obstruction or a wreck (Obstruction_point, _area and _line; Wreck_point and _area), an islet and its shoreline (Land_Area, Coastline_line) or a shoal patch (Depth_Area); a keep-out with no class, as in a synthetic world, is judged by its kind alone. A pile, a dolphin (Mooring_Warping_Facility), a bridge pylon, a hulk, a shore construction and every aid charted as a hazard point - a cardinal, isolated-danger, safe-water or special-purpose buoy, a safe-water or special-purpose beacon - is charted as 'a charted hazard' just as a rock is, and is the bank (read by kind alone, piles and dolphins were passed on their shore side and a west cardinal on its east); so is anything built - a pier, a float, a bridge support - and any kind of keep-out not named, however small: the chart serves no floats, so a pier head standing off on its own may be joined to the shore by one that is not drawn (on the real Piscataqua three charted piers 35-45 m across were read as rocks before this was said). The exception is a built thing the chart gives only as an obstruction, with nothing to say what it is: the extract keeps no CATOBS or CATFIF (asv_console.py ENC_KEEP_PROPS), so a fish weir (on Eastport's chart 22 Obstruction areas and 20 Obstruction lines have a Fishing_Facility twin) or a tidal turbine arrives as a plain Obstruction and is a rock, passable on either side - inshore of a weir too. Kept so for now by his call (2026-10-06, the rock review's RB1); no reach lane is laid in that water on any sampled path. Keeping CATOBS in the extract, or reading an Obstruction with a Fishing_Facility twin as the bank, would change it. A rock is no more than ROCK_MAX_M (150 m) across (the larger side of its bounding box; a cluster's, of their combined box), and rocks closer together than twice the floor are one; it has at least twice the floor of water to every other keep-out; and no lateral mark stands within ROCK_MARKED_M (50 m) of it: a hazard a mark stands beside is the edge that mark marks. Each of those distances is measured OUTLINE TO OUTLINE - the nearest two points of two outlines lie at a vertex of one of them, a point's own extent counted: measured from a circle round each bounding box, a shoal 100 m across with 40 m of water to the bank read as the bank. So a shoal off a bank, and a chain of rocks that clusters longer than ROCK_MAX_M, are the bank. The lane's banks (`koWall`) and what it reads between two rays leave the rocks out, and so do the hold past a reach's end and the shrink of each lane point clear of the keep-outs (`koKeep`). Once the line is laid, each rock it brings her nearer than its floor (less 0.5 m) - asked of the line AND of the route as it will be smoothed, since the smoothing rounds a bend in the river or the knee of the lane's ease sideways onto a rock the line cleared (0.2-3.9 m off, and the lane then lost to the backstop or shipped with a 45 degree dart) - and nearer than her own path was, is passed on whichever side is the smaller move from the line, by moving THE LANE'S OWN LINE: the far side by raising it over the window by what the samples abeam of the rock need (u, upBy) and those samples to the rock's own limit (only where every sample of its window has a cross-section - inside a reach - and within the bank's cap there), the near side by lowering it by what they need (d, downBy) - measured to the rock's limit even where that lies past her path, so a widening still lowers the approach - and capping the samples abeam at the rock's limit. (Capped flat at the rock's offset from her path instead, a rock on the lane's line where the chart's lane eases her path across the river toward a fairway held the lane 61-73 m toward her path for hundreds of meters before it - the rock review's gap round, 93v; moved, with its need clamped at her path, the widening stopped lowering the approach 33 m short of a rock nearer the fairway and the lane was lost - 93w.) A rock her own path passes inside its floor, passed on the near side, is passed as her path passes it: the lane comes no nearer her path than her path, the rounds stop there, and the route without the lane - smoothed and gated - passes it wider, so the backstop dropped the whole lane (four rocks of the fairway sweep); the lane now rides her path over the rock's whole window, once (`onPath`; 93x), which is what the flat cap did for such a rock. Its width across her line is read from its outline, each vertex and a point's own extent, so a shoal 15 m wide and 140 m long lying along her course is passed at its floor as a 15 m square is (read as its bounding box's half-diagonal it was 141 m wide, and passed 59 m off). The window is as far as its outline reaches along her route, its floor, two smoothing steps (2 x HOLD, HOLD = max(45, 13 x buffer)) and a sample step (STEP) either side of the sample abeam of it: at least 115 m and the floor beyond a point rock at a 3 m buffer, 165 m and the floor at 5. A far pass another rock's near cap has made impossible gives way to a near pass (two rocks either side of the line, one sent each way, held the first inside its floor through every round and the whole lane was lost; sides only ever change far to near, so the rounds converge); a pass the smoothing still cuts into is widened by what it cut; and the line is laid again, up to ROCK_ROUNDS (12) rounds - four left the fifth of a run of seven rocks inside its floor, and with the line moved rather than capped eight lost the lane on the same run (93n). What is left is the backstop's: `grazes`, in `finish`, judges a rock at its own floor. A rock the line already clears by its floor moves nothing, nor does one her own path already passes inside it where the line takes her no nearer. The floor is the model's (opts.rockModel: state.js V.REACH_ROCK, 'land' on a fresh page, which passage.js's channelLaneRoute hands to every Go-To, RTH, transit, survey approach and leg into a later survey). Land ('land', the default) is clr + STANDOFF, the lane's own clearance from a bank: a rock on the lane's line is passed 8.7 m off at a 3 m buffer, 11.9 m at 5 and 18.0 m at 8 (a pass within half a meter of its floor is not laid again). Shoal ('bottom') is clr + MS, the ordinary keep-clear and a march step, max(2, buffer / 2): 4.9 m at 3, 7.5 m at 5, 12.0 m at 8 - at the bare keep-clear the smoothing and the gate made a 31 degree jog of a 3 m step round a rock on the line. clr is the buffer, or the guard's standoff in a set: in a 12 m set at a 3 m buffer Shoal passes it 14.0 m off and Land 18.0 m. Twice the floor is what makes a cluster and what water all round means, so the two models can read the same rocks differently: two 15 m apart are one as Land at any buffer over 1.5 m (twice its floor is 18 m at 3), and as Shoal only over 5 m (twice its floor is 10 m at 3, exactly 15 m at 5, 18 m at 6). The survey card's Channel rocks row (Land or Shoal, under Turn shape) sets it; it changes no keep-out and rebuilds nothing - the next plan is laid with it - it is not saved with the mission, and the line table's transit and RTH estimate is keyed on it (transitEstKey), since a change of model is a different route. It is the floor of this lane only, by his call (2026-10-06: 'keep the rock rule to single marks only'; check 93y): in the chart's own channel (chartedChannelLane), between buoy pairs (buoyChannelLane) and in unmarked narrow water (narrowChannelLane) a rock is a keep-out like any other, passed at the ordinary keep-clear in either model, and the banks' lane may read one a ray passes within the buffer of as its edge. On the real Piscataqua his Go-To (the DriX H-8 at a 5 m buffer and a 3 m depth floor), both ways and in both models, keeps its 14 marks with none wrong, no foul leg and nothing inside the shipyard's restricted area; against the code before the rock models, as Land the route moves only at the 40 m islet short of Hen Island Ledge Buoy 1, which inbound she now passes on its north side, up to 90 m from the old line, and outbound moves up to 34 m near it; as Shoal it moves there the same and, inbound, also up to 12 m over 370 m beside an islet with two shoal patches about 1.1 km south-west of it - the bank as Land, with less than twice Land's 12 m floor of water all round it, a rock as Shoal - which she now passes 11 m nearer, still some 90 m off. Moving the lane's own line changed neither route by as much as half a meter. Fox Point is unchanged: the hazards charted there are oil-boom deployment buoys (special-purpose aids) and two piles, the bank before and after. Over 150 cached real search paths on four charts a lane is lost where rocks no longer narrow the water into a channel (Cape Canaveral #12; the Piscataqua at a 3 m buffer #7, where two small shoal patches the bounding circle read as the bank are rocks by their outline; Portland #16 loses only the flag, on the same route); as Shoal, Honolulu #14 for the same reason, and #18 to the turn test, which drops a lane whose sharpest turn is 50.7 degrees for the route without it, whose own 59.7 degree turn is 53 m away - further than NEW_TURN_NEAR_M (noted, not changed)."));
c.push(P("The single marks (markPassRoute). A mark is read from the numbered marks OF ITS OWN HAND nearest before and after it along her route (MARK_ROUTE_NEIGHBOR_M, step at most MARK_STEP_MAX, along her route in the plane AND in her own stations), its own named channel first - and a channel of its own name with no mark along her route CROSSES it. It is left alone, with the reason in plan.marks, as a junction mark, in charted water she rides, across land, unnumbered or with no neighbor, where the two readings disagree, where its channel goes on without her past it or she joins that channel only beyond it. A GATE PARTNER - the other hand's mark numbered one off within MARK_GATE_M, however her track crosses the line between them - is never read as the mark before or after, nor its channel as one she joins; and another named channel's numbers speak for a mark only where they continue its count - every one on one side of it, and numbered on FROM it: the count's mark nearest this one carries the count's number facing it, and the same the other way where the other is a single (continues, facing). A mark the pair lane rides is in her channel: 'goes on without her' and 'joins beyond' are not asked of it. A mark too near her route's start or end to be moved for is not moved (nearStart / nearEnd), and is excused the count too where it lies BESIDE her berth or her destination (no further along her route than MARK_BESIDE_RATIO, 1.5, times its distance off - one 900 m on from her start, too far off to be eased out to, had been excused); at a waypoint between two legs of a drawn transit it is COUNTED (markCounted with the leg's `ends`: excused the move, never the count). A buoy kept to starboard is passed at MARK_PASS_M (10 m, 2 x buffer, or the standoff and 2 m in a set), one kept to port at three times that, never across the pass line of a starboard buoy beside it; a beacon is never pulled close. Each pass is a straight run past the mark along the channel's course, or failing that along her own track there (MARK_TRACK_COS), put in ONE AT A TIME: kept only if every mark already on its proper hand still is - a mark it would cross, governed or left alone, brings its own run along, or the pass is refused - and no kept vertex turns her more than MARK_JOIN_MAX_DEG; marks on their wrong hand first, then close passes; where a mark would be left on its WRONG hand only because its pass cannot be joined, the pass before it (then the one after) and its own are laid again aimed at each other - buoy to buoy, the channel's course, where her own track is the search's shortest way - only where that line runs along her route (MARK_ALONG_COS: two marks abeam across the channel are a gate, and aimed at each other she crossed beam-on and ran back), and a pass refused for want of a join is asked again whenever the count placed changes, until a round places nothing (MARK_STAGE_ROUNDS); bounded by WORK - MARK_STAGE_LEGS legs routed out to a pass point and back, each by legPath's quick search (one wider region, no escape) - not by the clock, so the same Go-To plans the same route on a busy machine. The budget is a HARD STOP (his call, 2026-10-06, check 94): once it is spent no pass is tried again, not even the buoy-to-buoy re-aim - the mark is counted where it stands, on its wrong hand, and said (routed past the limit all the same, one re-aim cost 9 legs more, 93 of 80, and put The Rocks Buoy 4 right in the world built for it). A mark of the pair lane is 'ridden as a pair' only where that lane leaves it on its proper hand."));
c.push(P("OFFSHORE MARKS (2026-10-12; markPassRoute, offshoreDangerSide). Andy, on his Go-To past Thacher Island off Rockport: 'Path planning should have run the ASV between these buoys', and then: 'offshore buoys are to be handled differently than channel buoys. They are meant to guide vessels away from danger rather than route them down a constrained path'. Oak Rock Buoy 2 and Thacher Island Buoy 1, 335 m apart and each the only mark of its name, were both left alone ('direction of buoyage not known') and the route ran 90 m outside the pair. A mark whose direction is not read is now held until every mark has been read, and is an OFFSHORE mark where no channel lies about it - no mark within MARK_NEIGHBOR_M of it along her route with a direction read, none the pair lane rides, none in a charted channel (an unnumbered red in a buoyed reach is not one: enc_channel 95, 96b). Its hand is read off the chart: offshoreDangerSide takes every keep-out within OFFSHORE_DANGER_M (400 m) of the mark - not the buoys' own points, not a vessel's hull - by its nearest point, counts only those within OFFSHORE_BEAM_DEG (70) of her beam, and calls the nearer side the danger's where it is no further than OFFSHORE_SIDE_RATIO (0.6) of the farther; the mark is kept on THAT hand, between her and the danger. It is governed with `pull` off (never brought close), given MARK_PORT_BERTH times the calm-water pass distance on either hand, made no buoyed reach (buoyedReachLane's `known`; it never stands inside one, which runs between two channel marks nearer each other than MARK_NEIGHBOR_M), and counted apart (tallyMarks `offshore`, `offshoreWrong`; passage.js offshoreNote on the Go-To, RTH and transit banners; the Intent card says both whether or not a lane was ridden). AND A SINGLE READS A SINGLE OF ANOTHER NAME ONLY WITHIN MARK_SINGLE_NEIGHBOR_M (1,500 m) along her route (a mark of its own name as far as MARK_ROUTE_NEIGHBOR_M, 4,000 m, as before): on his route Thacher Island Buoy 1 had taken a direction from Avery Ledge Lighted Buoy 2, 3.2 km back, and was to be passed close on the island's side. Swept over his whole 61 km Go-To on his own chart: 8 offshore marks, every one on its safe hand, none wrong (Sandy Bay Breakwater 3, read before as a channel mark passed close to starboard, had its breakwater on that side). The 5.4-9.1 m patch about Oak Rock reaches further east of the buoy than its berth: keeping her off water that shallow is deep water first's (the next paragraph), which now keeps her between the pair and off the patch."));
c.push(P("THE WHOLE BERTH (2026-10-13, Andy: 'yes, hold offshore marks to the full 30 m berth'). An offshore mark's limit, `k.min` - nearer than which the marks stage moves her - is its berth (`k.want`, MARK_PORT_BERTH times the calm-water pass distance: 30 m at the DriX's 5 m buffer), where a channel mark's stays MARK_PASS_M. Live off Rockport deep water first's route passed Oak Rock Buoy 2 already on its safe side, 15 m off, and at MARK_PASS_M (10 m) the stage had nothing to move her for; it moves her out to the berth now (29.9 m). Where the water gives her less, the mark is counted `offshoreClose` (markVerdicts' `inside`: on its proper hand, nearer than its berth less MARK_BERTH_SLACK_M, 1 m) - said on the banner ('inside its berth') and warned on the Intent card. Deep water first treats a mark inside its berth as one on its wrong hand: its judge refuses a deeper way that squeezes past one, and its second asking holds one within DEEP_MARK_M of the margin layer itself, not only of the margin water her route ran in, with the layer lifted about it before any return - beside a band of margin water her own line never touched, the layer had kept the stage from every run out to a buoy's berth and held her 18 m off it. On his 61 km Go-To every one of the 8 offshore marks is passed at 30 m or more, none inside its berth."));
c.push(P("DEEP WATER FIRST (2026-10-12; keepouts.js deepKeepouts / deepReleased, passage.js armDeep / deepFirst / deepLanePass / deepSaid). Andy, of the same Go-To, which crossed the 5.4-9.1 m patch beside Oak Rock - legal water at the DriX's 4.0 m floor: 'There should be an awareness that although the ENC is an excellent source of data it can be inaccurate in some instances'. His choice, asked: plan first keeping 2 m over the floor and 50 m off charted rocks and shoal soundings, today's floor and buffer only on stretches with no other way, and say so on the plan. THE MARGIN LAYER (deepKeepouts, built beside the model as `ko.margin` by rebuildNogo, and by nogoModelAt at a passage section's own water): every depth or dredged area legal at the floor whose SHALLOWEST depth (DRVAL1, with the water) is under floor + DEEP_MARGIN_M (2 m) - the floor's own test reads a band by its deepest, so a 5.4-9.1 m band is legal at 4.0 - AND NO WIDER THAN DEEP_BAND_MAX_M (10 m; 2026-10-13, Andy: 'yes, build the 10 m band-width rule'): his Go-To from New Castle to Portland went 970 m round open water off Biddeford Pool, up to 837 m off the straight line, because the coastal chart's 0-18.2 m band (shoreline to the 10-fathom line) read as margin water by its 0, where the soundings within 300 m of the 3.0 km the line ran in it were 15.5 m and deeper; over his cached charts the candidate bands are up to 9.1 m wide or from 12.8 m, and an area with one bound is judged by it - and a berth of DEEP_BERTH_M (50 m) about every rock, obstruction, wreck and hulk no charted sounding clears floor + 2 m over (hazPassable at that floor), and about every sounding shallower than it, a sounding whose berth lies wholly inside an area dropped; the depth, hazard and wreck toggles followed. On the chart of his 61 km Go-To: 599 areas, 1,100 rocks, obstructions and wrecks, 1,608 soundings, built in 85-143 ms beside a model built in 59-68 ms. THE LAYER IS NOT A KEEP-OUT: `margin` is read by nothing. A planner ARMS it as `ko.deep` (armDeep: each berth's radius the berth less the margin the plan keeps, so 50 m is the distance kept at the buffer or the standoff), with a list of RELEASED regions; blocked, clearanceM and blockedInfo then refuse it outside those regions (deepReleased), and rasterKeepouts paints it on a grid of its own, clears only the cells a release holds whole (a cell and a half inside its edge: routing.js trusts a raster-clear leg, so this grid may never under-read), and folds it in. The armed layer is read through the model's own grid (A GRID OVER EVERY FEATURE LIST, in the route worker's section): walked whole, it had made each point test on his Go-To's chart 29 us dearer beside the floor model's 63 us. The guard, the punch, the hold disc and the home field never arm it. DEEP FIRST, ONE PASSAGE LEG AT A TIME (deepFirst; planNogoRoute, and routePlan on each transit leg): the floor's search runs first, as it always did - its refusal stands - and its route is walked at legClear's step against the layer alone. Each stretch of it in margin water (DEEP_JOIN_M 200 m apart is one) gets a window of its own, DEEP_ANCHOR_M (300 m) before and after it and never past the middle of the clear water to the next (one window for stretches whose windows met kept the Oak Rock pair's stretch, which has a deeper way, in margin water with the 1.3 km south of it, which had none), and the search's QUICK mode (one wider region, no escape rings: a deeper way that needs the whole ladder is not one worth having) is asked for a deeper way between its anchors on the armed model. It is TAKEN where it is no longer than DEEP_DETOUR (1.5) times the way it replaces, or DEEP_DETOUR_MIN_M (1 km) longer, whichever is more, and leaves no more marks wrong; otherwise the stretch is RELEASED - the layer lifted within DEEP_RELEASE_M (150 m) of the floor's own stretch - and kept at the floor. Where she starts or ends in margin water, the water about her out to the nearest deeper water (rings out to DEEP_REACH_M, 3 km) is released first, so she leaves it the short way. A BUOY OUTRANKS THE MARGIN, twice over. A deeper way within DEEP_MARK_M (400 m) of a mark is judged by the marks stage itself (markPassRoute), run on it and on the floor's way with DEEP_MARK_CTX_M (1.5 km) of route either side - both on the armed layer, the floor's way with its own stretch released, as it would ship (judged on the floor's own model, the floor's piece moved freely through the next stretch along, not yet released, and three stretches before the Dog Bar were held for what only the last one did) - and refused where it leaves wrong ANY mark the floor's way keeps, mark by mark (counted, a deeper way out of Eastern Point that kept Eastern Point Ledge 4, which the floor's way starting beside it does not, and passed the Dog Bar red on its bar's side was one for one, and taken): on his Go-To the deeper way into Gloucester ran west below the Dog Bar pair, turned her course at the red's station west, and the red, read by that course, was passed with the bar between her and it - and no reading of the route alone told that from a good case (the floor's own route passed Oak Rock 2 on its far side too, the marks stage moving it between the pair; her course turned 86 degrees at the Dog Bar red and 78 at Avery Ledge 2, which was kept). And where the lane pass (deepLanePass) still leaves a mark wrong within DEEP_MARK_M of margin water the route ran in or of a deeper way taken, it is run again with those marks held - no deeper way near them, and the layer lifted within DEEP_MARK_M of each, so the marks stage moves her there as it always did (on the Dog Bar's chart no deeper way was taken at all, and the layer alone kept the stage from the Dog Bar Channel green's proper hand) - and that plan ships where it leaves fewer marks wrong, or as many having put right a mark it held (a buoy traded for one the floor's own way leaves wrong is a trade made back); where it puts none of them right, the first stands, deeper water and all. Every stage after the search - the lanes, the marks, the smoothing, the gates, keepStandoff - plans on the armed model as deepFirst left it, so none takes her back into margin water the search did not have to. The hold point stays on the floor's model; maneuvers (flyThrough, maneuver) and a survey's own legs are laid as before. WHAT IS SAID (deepSaid): the margin water the route that SHIPS is in (marginRuns), each stretch with its reason - where she starts, where she ends, at a waypoint, no deeper way, a buoy's proper hand, not searched in DEEP_BUDGET_MS - on the Go-To, RTH and transit banners (deepNote: nothing where she keeps the margin the whole way) and the Intent card (deepWhy, asked of every plan: amber where a stretch lies between her start and her end, each with its position). AND ON THE CHART (2026-10-13, Andy: 'draw the margin water stretches on the chart'): each stretch carries its own line along the route that ships (marginRuns `pts`: where it begins, her route's vertices within it, where it ends) and `w1`, the first waypoint of the plan's route at or past its end (a transit's counted across its legs; a passage's next section re-indexed after the waypoints she had left, the last section's still ahead of her carried over - marginRunsAfter); setPlanIntent keeps them with the route array they were measured on (`planIntent.margin`), and render draws them (drawMarginStretches) while that array is the one drawn - a route the guard splices in or a resume cuts is a new array, and they are not drawn on it - each with `w1` not behind her: a wide translucent ORANGE band OVER the route and its waypoints (drawn under them, at zoom 13 the waypoint diamonds, 12 px wide and 4 px apart, hid the band whole: not one of its pixels was left on the canvas), labeled MARGIN NOT KEPT (AT HER FLOOR until 14 October 2026 - Andy asked what it really meant: it read as water at her floor's depth, where it is water in which the 2 m / 50 m margin was not kept; the banner and the Intent card say 'with the margin not kept' to match), its length and why. Live on the scratch console, the 1.14 km west of Thacher Island: 171 canvas pixels of the band over the diamonds in a strip 86 px long at zoom 13, about its length. A stretch's reason is the reason recorded for the stretch kept at her floor that lies on it, before a held mark nearby (deepSaid). The layer goes to the route worker packed in the model's own transferred array (route_job.js packKo; koCount counts it). MEASURED on his own chart at his settings: at Oak Rock, between the pair, none of her route within 200 m of the pair over the patch (51 m before) - 40 m east of Oak Rock 2 from north of Avery Ledge, 22 m live on the scratch console from nearer the pair (the offshore rule moves her to a mark's 30 m berth only when she passes it nearer than MARK_PASS_M or on its wrong hand); over his whole 61 km Go-To, 7 stretches - 5 taken round, the 1.3 km west of Thacher Island released (its deeper way 5.6 km against 1.7 km), and 88 m at the Dog Bar held for its marks - and the water within the margin fell from 2,981 m in six stretches to 1,340 m in two, every offshore mark still kept, for a route 1.0 % longer (61.09 to 61.71 km); planning time 16.6 s to 23.4 s on his whole 61 km Go-To (the mean of three alternating runs; most of it the marks stage judging the deeper ways near marks) and 0.3-0.8 s more on the 3.7 km Oak Rock leg - in the route worker, so the page stays live; since the grid over every feature list (2026-10-13), 4.1 s and 5.5 s."));
c.push(P("ENDS FIRST (14 October 2026; openwater.js searchEnds / endField / searchField's seeds and goals / spineOf's floor). The field's cell grows with the leg (MAX_DIM cells along its long side): 152 m on his 120 km Go-To from a Portland wharf, every cell within the search's 900 m snap of her blocked, so searchField could not start and the close-in route stood, whatever chart was read (replayed on his charts: the same on the box the page read and on the wider one it asked for). searchEnds labels the field's connected water and takes the one the router's route runs longest through; an end whose own free cell is not in it gets endField - the same clearance-weighted search on a fine field of its own (WAY_OUT_R_M 12 km each way, WAY_OUT_MAX_DIM cells: 27 m), run over the whole harbor, every cell of that water it reaches an EXIT carrying what reaching it cost - and searchField starts from all of them (`seeds`) and ends at the far harbor's entries (`goals`, through a virtual end node), so the way out is the one that does the whole leg best. (The first cut took the nearest exit by price - Hussey Sound, 22 m off a shoal - before the leg's own search had a say.) The router's own route out is kept only where the fine field finds no way (a 40 m inlet). spineOf now takes no shortcut nearer a hazard than the least clearance of the path it replaces. Replayed: 132.2 km against 128.6, 4.8 km within 250 m of a charted danger against 22.7, 16 turns against 29; open water's own route keeps 56.6 m or more, and the Rule 9 lane through Hussey Sound's buoyed channel passes a shoal at its edge 22 m off."));
c.push(P("OPEN WATER FIRST (2026-10-14; static/js/openwater.js, passage.js planNogoRoute / routePlan, coarse.js openWaterCosts). Andy, with a drawing of his Go-To from Castine to the Fox Islands Thorofare: 'I am noticing the ASV always tends to thread its way through islands/rocks/shoals to reach its destination on the most efficient way possible. This is technically a good solution, but it enhances danger when the chart may have a positional error or unmarked feature. For open ocean/or large bay, etc paths consider a more direct and less complex path as a default consideration'; while it was built, 'pay attention to marker buoys that should help safe passage calculations' and 'safety is always better than efficiency for path planning'. WHY THE ROUTER THREADS: legPath searches a long leg as routeAroundSeg does, the straight line cut into 2.2 km pieces each searched within 900 m of it, every cell costing its length and nothing else - a route bends round what lies on the bee-line and never looks at the open water beyond an island group, and the shortest way wins however close it runs. THE SEARCH (openWaterLeg): one A* over a raster of the whole leg - the router route's box widened by MARGIN_FRAC (35%) of the leg, 2 to 15 km, at most MAX_DIM (900) cells on its long side - where a meter costs 1 + WEIGHT (4) x (1 - TRUST (0.75) x trust) x (1 - d / CLEAR_M (1000 m))^2, d the cell's clearance from the conservative raster of the keep-outs at the leg's clearance; then a string pull that takes a shortcut between two turn points only where it is clear on the raster AND costs no more than the stretch of path it replaces (a rule on clearance alone let the first leg out of a harbor, where the path itself runs close, hug the shore for kilometers); each leg of that spine made exact by legClear, or else by legPath. It is TAKEN where its exposure - its length weighted by the same price - is strictly below (1 - GAIN_MIN (0.10)) of the router route's, at no more than (1 + DETOUR_MAX (1.0)) its length, at the first berth of BERTHS_FRAC (1, 0.5, 0.25 of CLEAR_M) that is; otherwise the router's leg stands exactly (strictly: a route with nothing near it has nothing to take away, and 0 <= 0.9 x 0 replaced one over open sea). The caps were 0.30 and 0.25 when first written, and were changed for 'safety is always better than efficiency': a cap refuses an absurd detour, not a long one. THE BUOYS (his rule since 2026-10-12: channel buoys define a path, offshore buoys guide away from danger): water a charted or buoy-gate channel covers (ko.chans), water within LATERAL_TRUST_R_M (250 m) of a lateral mark and of the reach between two marks of one system next in number (ko.sys) up to LATERAL_REACH_MAX_M (2.5 km) apart, and water within SAFE_WATER_R_M (400 m) of a safe-water buoy is TRUSTED; an isolated-danger buoy is a soft hazard ISOLATED_DANGER_R_M (150 m) plus its radius out; and a buoy's own nearness is never priced - every Buoy_ class and channel buoy is rasterized on a grid of its own after the clearance is measured, so it is blocked but repels nothing (routing.js's own rule: the marks are not what they mark). The first cut priced a lateral mark with no partner as a hazard and every buoy's nearness: on tests/enc_channel.js's worlds the route left a channel of single marks for open water a kilometer off every buoy. A BUOY OUTRANKS OPEN WATER (passage.js buoysOutrankOpenWater), as it outranks deep water first's margin: the stages after the search lay the marks on whatever way the search took, and on his 61 km Go-To from New Castle to Gloucester the wider way came into the outer harbor on a line that left Tenpound Island Ledge 6 on its wrong hand, 335 m off, where the close-in way kept it. So a leg taken in open water that leaves a mark wrong - or an offshore mark inside its berth, deep first's own fault - is planned again with the router's own route held within MARK_HOLD_M (1,500 m) of each such mark and open water either side (each side its own openWaterLeg on the router's own piece of route); that ships where it leaves none of them wrong and no new one (`held`, said on the banner and the Intent card); else the router's leg ships wherever it keeps any mark the wider way leaves wrong (`why: \"a buoy's proper hand\"`); else the wider way stands - the close-in way leaves the mark wrong too. Nothing is planned again unless a mark is left wrong. On the Gloucester Go-To it holds the close-in way past red 6 and leaves no mark wrong; the plan takes 40 s against 34 s in the route worker for the extra pass. ONLY WATER THE CHART WAS READ OVER (openWaterField `chart`): the model holds nothing past the box the chart was read over or in a square no chart covers, and nothing is what this search prizes most - so a cell less than CHART_EDGE_M inside nogo.bbox, or within it of an unread square (those within it of her own start aside, as beyondChart has them), is blocked for the search and the pull but is no danger, the clearance being measured before. And the page reads that water first (asv.html openWaterRead, from planInsideChart after the command's own points are judged and before the vessels along it and the plan): a leg of MIN_LEG_M (3 km) or more has the chart read over openWaterBox - its two ends' box widened by the search's own margin - joined to the water already read, a keep read (refreshNogo), then the plan's own water again (ensureNogoCovers); not where that water is already read, where the joined box is too wide for an extract (said in the log), or with the switch off; an Upload's approach leg alone. WHERE IT IS ASKED: planNogoRoute (Go-To, RTH, the route estimates, Continue's way back onto a survey; never a maneuver) -> plan.openWater; routePlan per transit leg, with `ropts.openWater` \"all\" (a plan's approach and transit legs; a passage's sections, sailSend) or \"detours\" (a drawn transit's default: only a leg the router bent round something, so the operator's own line stands where it is clear), never a survey line -> plan.openWater, a verdict per leg asked. route_job.js carries ropts to both searches, and V travels with each job. Everything after the search - deep water first, the Rule 9 lanes, the marks, the smoothing, the gate, the chart box - runs on whichever leg it is, unchanged. THE SWITCH: Route rules' Open water (First / Off; V.OPEN_WATER, true on a fresh page; Off asks nothing, the planners exactly as before). THE WORDS: openWaterNote leads the Go-To, RTH and transit banners' notes (the berth, and what it added or saved beside the close-in route; nothing where none was taken), and openWaterWhy gives the Intent card its line, and one for the legs asked that kept the close-in route, with why. THE COARSE PASSAGE PLANNER (coarse.js): buildField, unless opts.openWater is false, gives each tile `nr` - nearCost of each cell's chamfer clearance (center to center, at the tile's latitude, over a halo of the neighbor tiles CLEAR_M wide) from the nearest cell a danger touches or blocks, as a byte; null where nothing is near (open ocean tiles cost nothing more). cellCost adds OPEN_WATER.WEIGHT x it, except inside a port's circle (the detail planner's water); pullTaut keeps its level rule and adds the same cost rule as the detail spine, the shortcut's cost integrated along it against the path's own; coarseHomeField prices its cells the same, home's own circle excepted, so the fuel monitor's way home is measured as she would sail it (coarseHomeKey carries the switch, so a change rebuilds it). planPassage({openWater}) and the coarse worker's message carry it. MEASURED over his chart cache in the four areas he runs (tools/open_water_missions.js: the DriX H-8 EM712 at a 4.0 m floor, +0.5 m of water, a 20 m transit standoff; a tile not cached held as unread water; each route measured against the chart's dangers with its buoys aside): of 13 Go-Tos compared (replayed with ends first, 14 October 2026), open water was taken on 12 (Shoals to Rye kept: no safer); within 250 m of a charted danger 8.3 km against 71.3 km, within 100 m 0.9 km against 13.7 km, turns over 20 degrees 28 against 113, buoys on the wrong hand 0 against 1, for 5.1% more distance (376.2 km against 358.1; Castine to the Thorofare 10% shorter, Gloucester to Rockport 30% longer, outside Thacher Island). His Castine Go-To: 38.46 km against 42.50, 2.56 km within 250 m against 19.29, no turn against 41, crossing 44.15 N at 68.94 W - west of North Haven, as his drawing did - planned in about 4 s where the shortest way's own search of that leg took 113 s. Live on a scratch console, the same Go-To from the Path planning card: the read of the water it searches took the model from 3,322 to 9,325 zones (its rebuild held the page 6.7 s), the route worker's search 17.1 s, 483 waypoints, 37.52 km, and the banner and the Intent card said it. The coarse planner on his 40 cached grids, New Castle to Castine: 222.3 km against 224.9, its cells within two cells of a danger 99 against 385 and within five 195 against 809, planned in 2.9 s against 0.9 s. Guarded by tests/open_water.js, passage_plan 20-20c and fuel_rth 11i; 45 mutations, all killed."));
c.push(P("This applies to every mode — go-to, return-to-home, transit, the survey approach leg, inter-line transits, search transits and any routed detour. Survey coverage lines and generated survey turns are NEVER offset: they are planned geometry and must be run as planned."));
c.push(P("Where the lane lets go. A channel does not end at its last buoy, and neither may the lane: a vessel that releases at the final mark cuts back across the fairway exactly where converging traffic expects it to stand on. The channel centerline is therefore EXTENDED, straight along its own terminal axis, before the lane is built on it — as far as the charted channel polygon continues, or one full channel width past the final pair, whichever reaches further, and capped so a dredged area running far beyond the buoyage cannot drag the lane along water the marks never claimed. The offset is held at full value through that extension and released only after it, entering and leaving alike."));
c.push(P("What the console claims about it (plan.how, passage.js buoyageNote). Each part is claimed only where it stands on the route that SHIPS - after the smoothing, the gate and, in a set, the standoff re-gate: the chart's lane by chartedRide (the owned water the finished route crosses less than half way over, chartedShortM), the pair lane and the banks' lane only where their moves lie outside chart-owned water, the chart's lane not at all where more than half the water it owns was crossed short of it, the buoyed reach ('right of center in the buoyed channel') only where `finish` kept it and its moves lie outside chart-owned water - in a set, only where the standoff's re-gate kept it too - and the marks by marksKept on the finished route - a drawn transit's leg by leg, each leg's marks judged on that leg's own part of the shipped route (markVerdicts), every pass counted, and a mark beside the waypoint two legs share counted once - wrong if either leg's part leaves it wrong (routePlan, tallyMarks). In a set the standoff's re-gate re-measures the chart's ride on the route it ships (keepStandoff, chartedRide). The Intent card says how many of the buoys kept to starboard were passed close (marksKept's buoys and close). A mark on its wrong hand is said as that; PARTIAL is said for a stretch handed back to the router, a second buoyed channel neither kept mark by mark nor ridden by the chart, or charted water ridden short. A Rule 9 banner over a route that is not keeping right is worse than no banner, because it is a claim the operator would otherwise have verified. A maneuver - the way round a contact, back onto a line, back onto station - is laned as it always was: charted:false, marks:false, and pruneStitch without the stub rule."));
c.push(P("The gate's own two bounds (gateLegClear, 2026-10-04). The endpoint exemption covers the blocked water she is led out of, not the leg that leads her: the run of blocked samples contiguous with a blocked start (or goal) along the route, never nearer what blocks it than that endpoint less a quarter of the buffer - or, in a gate whose margin stands above the law the route has already passed (the standoff's re-gate, opts.floor), than that floor; past it the leg is checked like any other. A patch out of (into) a blocked endpoint is routed at the endpoint's own clearance less a quarter of the margin, no nearer than the floor, and knot-pruned there - at the full margin legPath snapped the endpoint to a cell behind it and the patch hooked round it. And legPath refuses a route whose leg into (out of) an endpoint inside its margin runs through a keep-out beyond the water that endpoint lies in (clearButEnds, END_CLEAR_M): the snapped goal of a resume-from-here lay across a point of land, and its last leg ran over 12 m of it. (From a berth inside the buffer the first block along ANY leg out of it is the berth itself, and the whole leg was waved through - past a pile 1.7 m off with a 3 m buffer, and through a dolphin 28 m out.) And a patch longer than GATE_PATCH_RATIO times the search's own way between the same two points, plus GATE_PATCH_SLACK_M, is not taken: that way is spliced instead, or the lane is given up where it cannot be joined (two lane points 89 m apart were once patched 10.6 km round the land)."));
c.push(P("Every route is flyable as well as lawful. Where the clearance gate splices a re-routed patch into the lane, the seam can fold a reversal only a few meters long — a waypoint demanding a near-180-degree turn in less water than any hull can turn in, which a vessel under way answers by orbiting the waypoint until it happens to capture it. The lane producer therefore prunes its final output: any waypoint whose corner exceeds what a transit needs AND whose neighbors connect clear of every keep-out is dropped. The clearance condition means the prune can never straighten a corner that exists to avoid an obstacle — it removes only the folds that no vessel could follow anyway."));

// 8 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H2("7.6  The clearance guard's look-ahead on a plan"));
c.push(P("On a plan the guard's look-ahead does not fly a straight line (static/js/guard.js projectRoute, handed the route by asv.html guardTrack): it walks the boat down the route the vessel is steering, from the waypoint the vessel reports it is steering toward (`wp_index`, in every state frame), swinging the bow at the hull's turn rate (`maneuvering.max_turn_rate_deg_s`), crabbing it into the set as the vessel's line-follower does, and advancing waypoints by the vessel's own two tests (asv_console.py, the plan tick): inside the approach radius, or past it along the leg. The walk ends where the route ends and invents no continuation."));
c.push(P("THE FIRST TARGET IS JUDGED BY THOSE SAME TWO TESTS BEFORE THE FIRST STEP, FROM WHERE THE LEG BEGAN (2026-10-06, Andy's two holds at Little Bay). Every 4 Hz state frame carries the vessel's index and the vessel advances it once a tick, so a frame can name a waypoint she is at, or up to 1.8 m past at 14 kn (a late tick, more). The walk asked the two tests only after its first step and measured along-track from her own position, so a waypoint 0.6 m astern read as not yet reached: the bow was swung back toward it and the model boat flew a loop astern, into a charted rock's 50 m disc 30 m abeam, on a Go-To whose route passed that rock 26 m off - a HOLD at 13.6 kn (10:25:04; with the next index the same frame reads clear) - and a 4 s slow-down on the Return-to-Home at a 28 degree bend 2.3 km away, off another charted rock (11:03:33). guardTrack now hands `prev`, where the leg began, and projectRoute consumes every leading target the vessel's rule already counts as reached before it steps; a route every waypoint of which is reached ends clear (the motion is over - the hold point is hold.js's to certify; a boat already inside a hazard there answers t = 0, as everywhere). Where the leg began is the vessel's `_seg_start`: the waypoint before the index once she has advanced onto a leg, but HER OWN POSITION at a start and at every amendment (SimVcu.start, amend_plan) - the page records that position from the command's reply (legStartsHere) and keeps it until the index moves (trackLegStart, from onState). Without it the backtrack point a Pause/Resume splices in - astern of her, between the waypoint before and her - read as already passed from that waypoint, and the look-ahead ran up the line while she turned to run twelve lengths down it: a false hold on anything beyond the pause mark, and a miss on the water she turns through (the review's finding SR-2, the same day). Without `prev` at all her own position stands in, and only a target inside the approach radius can be read as reached."));
c.push(P("AND A PLAN'S INDEX BELONGS TO ITS PLAN. A Go-To, Return-to-Home, transit or re-approach the vessel takes answers with a state frame carrying the NEW plan's `wp_index` and `wp_total` before the command's handler has resumed and swapped the drawn route (`runRoute`); the automatic re-plan after a chart read (planInsideChart) sent a 103-point Go-To while the old 106-point one ran at 13.7 kn, and the console sliced the old route at the new plan's index 0 - a waypoint 80 m astern - looped into a second charted rock's disc, one that route passed 5.9 m off, and held her 80 ms after the vessel took the new plan, which the hold then replaced (10:32:45). Two gates, each seeing what the other cannot. While one of those four commands is in flight (routeCmd counts the POST from send to reply) guardTrack projects no route: the frame answering it is the new plan's whatever its length. And a frame whose plan is not the length of the route the page holds is another plan's index however it got there; the straight projection stands in, as for any boat not on a plan. The length alone was the first version of this rule, and the review found it could not see two plans of the same length in the swap window. For the lengths to agree the page and the vessel must splice an amendment at the same waypoint: the page plans the tail at the index it read from its last frame, the vessel splices at its LIVE index when the amendment lands, and when she advanced a waypoint in between the two differed by one - the drawn route one shorter than the plan aboard for the rest of the motion, and the guard with no route to walk. So SimVcu.amend_plan returns the index it spliced at, Engine.amend sets `wp_total` from it in the reply (and drops the frame read across the command), and the page splices at the reply's count less the tail (spliceAt) - where the vessel did. A frame with no `wp_total` (an older console) projects as before. Pinned by track_edge 28-28c, clearance_guard 19-19g and amend_plan 10b; the replay of the recording through the page's own modules is in the handoff."));
c.push(H1("8  Survey planning and turn geometry"));
c.push(H2("8.1  Pipeline"));
c.push(P("A three-click pattern (start corner, opposite corner, then a spacing and direction point) generates parallel lines filling the box. A center MOVE GRIP translates every anchor by one delta, so the whole pattern moves with shape, spacing and direction preserved exactly. An optional boundary polygon clips the lines. Punch Out then trims everything to chart-clear water:"));
c.push(B("Clip every line to nogo-clear water, sampling at buffer resolution."));
c.push(B("Re-order the result so each obstacle-free region is run as its own serpentine, and no leg is numbered through a keep-out (8.3)."));
c.push(B("Shorten each segment at both ends to settle on-line and leave turning room."));
c.push(B("Drop any segment shorter than this hull's minimum survey line and bridge straight to the next one. A line costs two turns whatever its length, so below some length the vessel spends longer maneuvering onto it than surveying it — and that length is a property of the hull, so it is vessel configuration (planning.min_survey_line_m), not a constant. The length judged is the segment AS RUN, after the turn margin has been taken off both ends, because that is the water actually surveyed. Zero, or an absent setting, keeps every line: a small survey launch is built for exactly the short lines a large vessel should skip. Whatever is dropped is reported in the punch-out readout with the count and the threshold — coverage is never removed silently. Surveys only; transits, search patterns and hand-drawn lines are never filtered."));
c.push(B("Generate a turn at every line-to-line reversal (below)."));
c.push(B("Route the remaining transits: straight if clear, else around the obstacle with the channel lane applied, else flag them."));
c.push(H2("8.2  Turn geometry"));
c.push(P("Every generated turn is built at a radius the vessel can actually HOLD at the plan speed — derived from the vessel file's maximum turn rate, with a margin for line-following overshoot. There are two shapes, and both are validated against the nogo model before use."));
c.push(TBL(["Shape", "When", "Geometry"], [
  ["Semicircle", "Line offset ≥ 2 × minimum radius", "One 180° arc of radius half the offset, bulging outboard; reaches exactly that radius past the line ends"],
  ["Teardrop", "Line offset < 2 × minimum radius", "Three tangent circles AT the minimum radius: a short arc away from the next line, a >180° loop over the top, a short arc onto the line"],
], [1500, 2800, 5060]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("DECOUPLING THE TURN RADIUS FROM THE LINE SPACING is the point of the second shape. A large, slow-turning vessel may need more radius than half the line spacing can ever supply; before the teardrop existed, such a vessel got no turn at all and the plan fell back to a straight hop between anti-parallel line ends — which is a 180° reversal at half the spacing, precisely the radius that had just been rejected as unflyable, only now unmodeled."));
c.push(P("The teardrop costs outboard water: up to roughly 2.75 times the minimum radius past the line ends, against one radius for the semicircle. Punch Out therefore reports the actual excursion, the spacing a plain semicircle would need at the plan speed, and the spacing needed at the slowest whole knot the hull offers, so widening the lines or slowing down stays the operator's decision. Where no rung of the turn ladder fits, the pair gets no turn and is flagged red as UNSAFE, and Add to plan is refused while it stands (8.3.3)."));
c.push(P("At exactly twice the minimum radius the teardrop's middle circle degenerates and the two shapes are the same semicircle, so the families agree on their shared boundary."));
c.push(P("TURN WATER. The keep-out set the turns are validated against is STRICTER than the one the transits use: channel polygons (ENC dredged areas and fairways, plus buoy-gate corridors where the chart charts no channel) that contain no sample of any clipped coverage line are added as keep-outs for the reversal turns, the reversal straight-hop check and the serpentine ordering. The lines' own water is the plan's declared water; a loop may not enter a navigation channel the survey does not occupy, even though a dredged area is deep, is not an enforced keep-out, and a charted pile row (individual point features) cannot block a loop that passes between the points. Routed region-hop transits keep the ordinary keep-out set — crossing a channel to reach the other side is lawful navigation and rides the channel-lane machinery. The grant is per polygon: a survey inside a channel keeps each fragment its lines actually reach. A refusal names the channel in the banner, outlines it as a violation, and quotes the standoff the turns need."));
c.push(P("BOTH CHANNEL RULES, AND THE CHARTED WRECKS, ARE THE OPERATOR'S TO DISREGARD (2026-09-30). The survey card's enforcement list carries two more boxes, both armed on a fresh page: `Shipwrecks` (`wreck`) and `Shipping channels` (`chan`). They ride in the shared enforcement object (`nogo.enf`, validated by `enforcement()` in keepouts.js, which refuses an unknown key, so a misspelled option throws instead of leaving every wreck armed). `wreck` off drops `Wreck_point` and `Wreck_area` in `buildKeepouts` after every gate that would have dropped them anyway, so it only ever narrows: a wreck its own class has switched off (`haz` for points, `land` for areas) is not counted, a wreck a sounding proves passable is counted as `passed`, and the model returns `wrecksOff` so the row, its tip and every punch can say how many the toggle took out. Every behavior and the clearance guard read the one model, so a disregarded wreck is disregarded everywhere. `chan` is read by punchOut alone: off, it skips the span exclusion and the turn water above, and the transits keep their Rule 9 lane whatever it says. The punch memo's key names `nogo.enf` whole, so a clip taken under one setting is never served under the other."));
c.push(P("A BUOY CORRIDOR YIELDS TO THE CHART (2026-09-30). `channelPolys` is the one list of channel polygons: the charted dredged areas and fairways, then a corridor for each buoy gate `pairGates` makes (a port mark and its nearest starboard mark 15-400 m away). It used to sweep every gate's line a full gate width each way along the channel axis. At the Northward Channel (Portsmouth) buoys #3 and #4 stand 244 m apart either side of a charted strip about 20 m wide, so the corridor held the strip and both banks. The span rule takes the UNION of the list, so a survey line drawn across the charted channel began and ended inside the corridor, read as contained, and was never cut. Five of that extract's seven gates bracket charted water, three of them pairing marks of two different channels, and the one gate at Lewes, the Roosevelt Inlet jetties, does too. `gateSweep` now sweeps a corridor only until it would first touch a charted ring. In the gate's own strip coordinates the first contact is an endpoint of a ring edge clipped to the strip, so the answer is exact and linear in the edges. A gate whose own line touches charted water sweeps nothing; a corridor touching none is unchanged, vertex for vertex; and an inlet mouth beyond a charted end keeps its seaward corridor, stopped AT the charted end. Over the live extracts: Portsmouth 11 charted rings and 2 corridors, Lewes 15 and none, about 0.4 ms a call. `channelLaneRoute` reads the same list for the Rule 9 scope and the centerline extension. Routes through both harbors were compared old against new and are identical; the lane engaged on the Lewes routes, and on none of the Portsmouth paths tried, under either list."));
c.push(P("A GATE IS TWO MARKS OF ONE CHANNEL (2026-10-01). `pairGates` took each port mark's nearest starboard mark whatever channel it marked. At Portsmouth four of the seven gates crossed channels: three Sagamore Creek and Northward Channel pairs where the two meet, which `gateSweep` already dropped as bracketing charted water, and a Goat Island Ledge and Henderson Point pair 307 m across, whose 612 x 678 m corridor no charted channel touched, so it stood. Seven survey lines drawn across it had a channel cut out of them; with the fix, none. A mark now pairs only with marks of its own channel: its name less its tail (`markId`), grouped by the same prefix merge the systems use. That merge is `markRoots` now, ONE rule read by `markSystems` and by `pairGates`; its output was checked identical to the old body on 20,000 randomized mark sets and on the real marks of Portsmouth, Lewes and Bellingham. Unnamed marks pair only with each other. After the fix Portsmouth makes 5 same-channel gates and keeps 1 corridor (Little Harbor's); Lewes and Bellingham pair exactly as before, and their lane routes, which engage there, are identical."));

c.push(H2("8.3  From plan to route: the running order"));
c.push(P("The order a survey is run in is fixed when the plan is COMMITTED and is never re-derived afterwards. `mission.waypoints` IS the running order: Upload routes it without re-ordering it, and the vessel steps through it index by index. What decides that order is the pattern generator, Punch Out's ordering pass and the pair loop behind it, in that sequence. `tests/survey_order.js` pins every rule below with the page's own functions."));
c.push(H3("8.3.1  The drawn pattern"));
c.push(P("`surveyPattern(A, B, C, align)` puts line 1 at the start corner A, moved across by the Align setting's share of the leftover width, on the heading az(A→C) − 90°, and reversed when B lies behind A on that heading; its length is the diagonal's along-track component. The line count is ⌈|width| / spacing⌉, capped at `MAX_SURVEY_LINES`. Each further line is the one before it moved one spacing TOWARD B — along az(A→C) when B lies on C's side of line 1, against it when B does not — and REVERSED. The drawn pattern is therefore already a serpentine that starts at A. The boundary polygon (`patSourceLines`) and the keep-out clip (`clipLine`, sampled at a third of the buffer) cut lines into RUNS without changing their direction, and `keptRuns` removes the runs struck off by hand before anything is ordered."));
c.push(H3("8.3.2  The order: Boustrophedon Cellular Decomposition"));
c.push(P("`regionOrder` (static/js/passage.js) orders the kept runs:"));
c.push(B("PROJECTION. Every run is projected onto the pattern's axes: ALONG the nominal line heading (`sp.direction`) and ACROSS it, positive to starboard of that heading, which is toward C as seen from A. A run's line index is round((across − the smallest across) / spacing). Index 0 is therefore the outermost line on the side AWAY from C."));
c.push(B("CELLS. A sweep in ascending line index builds coverage cells. A run joins an open cell only when, along-track, it overlaps exactly that one open cell and that cell overlaps no other run on the same line; a split, a merge, or a run that starts a new stretch closes the cells involved and opens a new one. A line index with no runs at all is simply not visited, so the cells on either side of a struck or wholly clipped line still join."));
c.push(B("FIRST CELL. The cell holding the lowest line index, ties broken by the smallest along-track start. Its first run is flown as it was handed in."));
c.push(B("NEXT CELL. Whichever remaining cell has ANY run end nearest the previous exit. This is a greedy nearest-neighbor choice, not an optimal tour, so a cell passed over early is reached last, with a hop back to it."));
c.push(B("INSIDE A CELL. Runs are taken in ascending line index, each entered at whichever of its ends is nearer the previous exit — the serpentine. The sweep always starts at the cell's lowest line, even when the greedy choice was made on a run further in."));
c.push(P("CONSEQUENCE, MEASURED: when B lies on C's side of line 1 the punched survey starts on line 1 at A, exactly as drawn. When it does not, index 0 is the pattern's LAST line, and the punched survey starts on the far side of the box and works back toward A — with an even line count, from the far end of that line. An un-punched plan always starts at A. `regionOrder` also returns `unsafe`, the exit-to-entry hops its `legSafe` refuses; `punchOut` does not use it, because the pair loop re-judges every consecutive pair itself."));
c.push(H3("8.3.3  After the order, in punchOut"));
c.push(P("Every run is shortened by the turn margin, max(2 m, half the line spacing), at both ends. Runs of 1 m or less, and runs shorter than `planning.min_survey_line_m`, are then dropped; the survivors keep their order. The lead-in and lead-out are added (`extendLead`, against the turn-water model). Then each consecutive pair k → k+1 is joined:"));
c.push(TBL(["Pair", "Test", "Joined by"], [
  ["Reversal", "Headings 180° ± 50° apart; the two lines closer ACROSS than (GAP_LINES + 0.6) spacings + 3 m + the larger lead, with GAP_LINES = 4 — so a reversal across a missing line still counts — AND the two ends within twice the reversal-pair cap, which is the furthest a generated turn can reach. Measuring the ACROSS separation is what admits a staggered pair, two neighboring runs the chart clipped to different extents; bounding the span is what stops one being admitted that the turn ladder would then have to refuse, which would flag it red and refuse the whole pattern", "A generated turn from `turnWithRetry` (8.2), the leads giving way first if nothing fits; when every rung is refused the pair is flagged red, and Add to plan is refused (below)"],
  ["Hop", "Anything else: a change of cell or region, a skewed pair, a gap too wide", "Nothing, if the straight leg is clear of the turn-water model; else a detour from `routeAround` passed through the lane pipeline with `lane:false` and the junction-knot prune (amber); else flagged red"],
], [1500, 3800, 4060]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("THE RED IS ENFORCED AT ADD TO PLAN (Andy's call, 2026-09-16). Every red join is recorded in `patRed` as {run, turn, why, by, a, b}, pushed in the same statement as its `patUnsafe` pair: `turn` marks a REVERSAL every rung refused, with `why` the first rung's reason from `turnWithRetry` and `by` the kind of keep-out `firstBlockAlong` found; otherwise it is a HOP with no straight leg and no detour. `punchRefusal()` refuses while any reversal stands. `updatePatReadout` grays out the button, puts the reason in its tooltip and in `#sp_refuse` under it — inside the panel the controls window mirrors — and `commitPattern` asks again AFTER flushing any pending re-punch, changing nothing when refused. The text names the pairs (right-click, What is this line?, identifies a red join by its runs), the causes, and only the remedies that can work: pull the line ends back, or strike a run, always; the low TURN speed only for a `track` refusal or a teardrop plan whose spacing the low-speed radius fits; a wider spacing only below twice the minimum radius; standoff where a navigation channel refused the turn. Never the lead — the give-way ladder has already tried the pair with none."));
c.push(P("A red HOP does not refuse: `legPath` at Upload searches further than `routeAround` (wider regions, then escape rings) and refuses the upload itself if it still finds no path. Two more refusals keep the gate honest. `patJoined` is false from the moment a punch starts replacing `patClip` until its pair loop has finished, so a punch that throws part-way is refused as unfinished. And `patDropped` records a punch the page threw away itself: `applyWaterOffset` drops `patClip` on any change of level, and the un-punched pattern — lines as drawn, no turns — must not be committed in its place, so the refusal stands until Punch Out produces runs again (a punch that finds no chart coverage, and `resetPattern`, also clear it; a level change with no punch on the chart records nothing)."));
c.push(P("BEFORE 2026-09-16 NONE OF THIS WAS ENFORCED: `patUnsafe` was drawn and bannered, `commitPattern`'s `resetPattern` cleared it, the committed plan kept a straight leg between the two line ends, and Upload found that leg clear of the model and sent it — on Andy's Honolulu plan that evening, 17 of 18 refused reversals went to the vessel as single straight legs, on both uploads. `tests/turn_refusal.js` drives the refusal end to end through a real punch. The punched plan is `patClip` (the runs, in order), `patLead` (their leads) and `patTransits` (the joining points of each pair)."));
c.push(H3("8.3.4  Committing"));
c.push(P("`commitPattern` first asks `punchRefusal` (8.3.3) and commits nothing when refused. Otherwise it APPENDS to the plan; it never replaces or re-sorts it. For each run it adds one `mission.lines` entry ({a, b, lead_in_m, lead_out_m}), then the run's two ends to `mission.waypoints`, then that pair's joining points flagged `turn: true`. An un-punched pattern commits its lines in drawn order, with no joining points and no leads. Several patterns added in turn run in the order they were added, and the hop from one to the next is joined only at Upload. PHASE 3a (2026-10-02) adds `patReverse` (the card's Backwards box, recorded as `settings.reverse`): `punchOut` replaces `ro.ordered` with `runBackwards(ro.ordered)` before the margins, leads and turns are built. `repunchSurvey` (Punch all, the row's Backwards) loads a survey as ✎ does, awaits `punchNow`, and commits through `commitPattern` only when `patClip` is set and `punchRefusal` passes - an unfinished punch would otherwise commit the drawing un-punched. Each committed survey records `water_m` and `punched_at`. Line progress (`trackLineCoverage`, after `accumLineTime` in the telemetry tick) is kept per line geometry in localStorage. PHASE 3b, THE HOLD BEFORE A SURVEY: a survey's `hold` flag splits `doUpload`'s waypoints at that survey's first waypoint (`splitAtHold`), and that upload asks `Engine.upload` for `completion: 'loiter'` - the only override it accepts - so the RUN ends in a loiter while the setting is untouched; `pendingHold` (localStorage) records it, `continueHold` uploads `{fromSv, release}` and calls `startRun` (the Start button's own body); `heldSplitRun` keeps the end-of-plan RTH chain and `rthPending` off a hold's split (a survey run ending in a loiter the setting does not ask for, WITH a pending hold - the same frame without one is a plan switched to RTH mid-run and still chains); `tailEndsAtHold` keeps a guard-held resume of the way there ending in the loiter. A partial plan (from a survey, or split) is always sent as its own route, the unrouted branch included. THE ONE EXCEPTION (2026-10-02, sequenced surveys phase 2): while the survey table's ✎ has a survey loaded (`editingSv`), `commitPattern` lifts back out what its loop appended and puts it WHERE THAT SURVEY'S ENTRIES WERE (`replaceSurvey`), keeping its id, number and name; ▲ ▼ (`moveSurvey`) swap a survey's blocks in both lists, and ✕ (`removeSurvey`) takes them out - free waypoints never move. Each survey records `settings` ({boundary, max_depth_m, lead_mode, lead_in, lead_out, turn_ease}, `surveySettingsNow`) for ✎ to put back (`applySurveySettings`); nothing the boat flies reads them, and `_check_survey_settings` checks their types only. EACH COMMITTED PATTERN IS A SURVEY (2026-10-02, sequenced surveys phase 1, static/js/surveys.js): `commitPattern` lists it in `mission.surveys` ({id, no, name, pattern: {A, B, C, align} | null, punched}) after the surveys already there, and tags every line and waypoint it pushed with `sv` = its id. `no` is one past the highest the plan holds and stays with the survey - never re-used in the plan, never renumbered by order. A waypoint with no tag (WPT, a search pattern) is FREE and belongs to no survey; each survey's entries are contiguous in both lists, in the listed order. `loadMission` carries `surveys` through its whitelist (as `load_mission` and `save_mission` do) and judges the plan with `normalizeSurveys`: lines and no surveys (a plan from before) becomes one survey S1, everything tagged; a sound plan is kept, its list put in run order; a broken grouping (an untagged line, a tag naming no listed survey, a survey's entries split, the two lists in different orders) is folded into the first-listed survey and bannered PLAN LOADED AS ONE SURVEY. `check_plan_body` checks the types only. A drag keeps a waypoint's fields (its survey, its `turn` flag), and `_keep_previous_plan` ignores `sv`, so tagging a plan takes no backup slot. Plan editing never re-orders either: a waypoint clicked in `WPT` mode is appended at the end, and `deleteLineByIndex` removes a line's two ends and leaves the joining points on either side — so the vessel still travels that line's track, from one turn to the other, as an uncounted leg. Striking the run off BEFORE committing re-punches around the gap instead. NOTHING RE-CHECKS THE TURNS AFTER COMMIT: the refusal is a property of the punch, and the committed plan carries no record of it, so a joining point deleted in `WPT` mode reshapes a checked turn into an unchecked one, and deleting all of a turn's points leaves the straight 180° the refusal exists to keep out. Deleting either line of a pair cannot restore a turn either — the remaining joining points still meet a line end at zero radius. A plan committed before 2026-09-16 may carry refused reversals as straight legs. In each case: CLR PLAN, draw the pattern again, Punch Out."));
c.push(H3("8.3.5  Upload"));
c.push(P("`doUpload` hands `mission.waypoints` (or, given `{fromSv}` by the survey table's Upload from, `waypointsFrom(mission, fromSv)` - refused when empty, before the empty-plan branch) and the vessel's fix at that moment to `routePlan`, which walks the waypoints in order. Each consecutive pair goes through `legPath`: kept as it is when the straight leg is clear, a routed detour inserted when it is not. Only leg 0, the APPROACH from the fix, goes through the Rule 9 lane - and, since 2026-10-02, the leg into each later survey (`routePlan`'s `transitAt`, from `surveyEntries`: found at the standoff and re-gated at it like the approach); a pattern's own legs stay on their planned track (every leg is laned only for a pure transit). A leg with no clear path blocks the upload and names the obstruction. What goes to the vessel is positions only — the `turn` flags are not sent — checked against `route_max_wpts`, with `hold_clear_m` for the last point, and `Engine.upload` loads it at the transit speed. A LINE leg that the keep-out model now blocks, because the buffer or the water level has changed since the punch, gets a detour spliced into it like any other leg — and then no longer reads as that line while it runs. Re-punch instead."));
c.push(H3("8.3.6  Running it"));
c.push(P("The simulator follows each leg from the previous waypoint with its look-ahead line follower and advances when it is within the approach radius of the waypoint or has passed it along-track. At the last waypoint the completion applies: Loiter and RTH hold there (and for RTH the console then chains the routed return), Repeat loops from the present position, Complete stops. While the run is under way the console classifies every leg the same way for the speed governor, the LINES table and the activity line: a SURVEY LINE only when both of the leg's ends are within `LINE_MATCH_M` (5 m) of a committed line's ends, in either direction (`currentLegLine`) - the NEAREST such line since 2026-10-02 (`lineOfLeg`, the one rule `currentLegLine` and `legOfLine` share: the smallest worst end distance, a resumed remainder by its end; the first match used to win, and on a pattern spaced 5 m or less the line before, run the other way, came first - at 4 m every line after the first was credited to the one before it, and Resume from here could run a line backwards; `committedRoleLengths` takes each line's ends as a PAIR, so a turn point near a line's start does not stop the plan-time walk); otherwise a TURN when a line has just been run, the gap to the next line is a reversal (`isReversalGap`) and the vessel is near a line end; otherwise a TRANSIT — the approach, or a hop between regions. EACH SURVEY IS JUDGED BY ITS OWN LINES (2026-10-02): `reversalScaleM(k)` and `turnZoneM(k)` measure only the pairs of lines inside line k's survey (untagged lines are one survey), so a tightly spaced survey no longer sets the scale for a wide one, and `isReversalGap` answers false for any gap BETWEEN two surveys - the leg to the next survey is a transit, as the operator's rule says. `linePartContinues` never joins two surveys' lines into one drawn line, and `lineSetKey` carries the tag. A plan that is one survey is judged exactly as before."));

// 9 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("9  Remote Operations Centers and moving HOME"));
c.push(P("The operational paradigm: humans command the vessel from one or more Remote Operations Centers. A ROC has a position and a telemetry link. Two kinds exist and a mission may use both."));
c.push(B("SHORE ROC — near the launch harbor, or anywhere. A usually fixed point. The real launch and recovery point is OFFSET from the antenna by an operator-entered range and bearing."));
c.push(B("SHIP ROC (mothership) — aboard a vessel near the mission area. A MOVING point. Recovery happens at a range and bearing FROM the ship, so the offset bearing is normally taken RELATIVE to the ship's course."));
c.push(H2("9.1  Arrival point"));
c.push(P("Each ROC's arrival point is its position walked out along its offset. A `true` offset is a compass bearing; a `relative` offset is measured from the ship's course, so “50 m astern” stays astern as the ship turns. The operator selects one ROC as HOME; the Engine resolves it every telemetry tick, so when HOME is a ship it MOVES and return-to-home chases it."));
c.push(H2("9.2  Lifecycle"));
c.push(P("PLACE → EDIT → CONFIRM. A ROC is created by clicking the chart and comes up STAGED: its position, and for a ship its heading and speed, are edited while it sits still. CONFIRM makes it ACTIVE — a shore ROC becomes a usable HOME anchor, a ship starts steaming. HOLD stops a ship and returns it to STAGED. Only an ACTIVE ROC may be selected as HOME; the gate is a safety property, not decoration."));
c.push(H2("9.3  The chase"));
c.push(P("While a return-to-home follows a ROC, the run loop re-aims the vessel at the ROC's current arrival point, re-issuing a fresh single-waypoint plan only once the point has drifted past about half the arrival radius. This reuses the link's own waypoint following rather than introducing a separate pursuit controller. Any command that leaves the return-to-home run clears the chase."));
c.push(P("A ROC home is driven direct rather than by a chart-routed detour, because a detour computed to a moving recovery point would be stale by the time the vessel arrived."));
c.push(H2("9.4  Vessel-dependent behavior"));
c.push(P("Two ROC values are derived from the active vessel and re-derived on a live vessel switch:"));
c.push(B("RECOVERY STANDOFF — the default astern offset for a new ship ROC scales with the hull (six lengths, with a small-boat floor) and may be overridden outright by `planning.roc.ship_recovery_m`. A fixed distance cannot serve both a two-meter vessel and a twenty-meter one."));
c.push(B("CLOSING CHECK — a return-to-home against a moving mothership only converges if the vessel can overhaul it. The console subtracts the ship's speed from the vessel's best speed and reports the closing rate; below a small margin the recovery point is marked unreachable and the run note says so. The same mothership speed is routine for a fast vessel and impossible for a slow one, so this cannot be a constant."));
c.push(H2("9.5  Position sources"));
c.push(P("A ROC position arrives three ways, all funnelling into one feed path: manually from the card, pushed by any external process to the API, or read from a real NMEA-0183 GPS stream over TCP or UDP. Sentences are checksum-verified and a void fix is rejected — a GPS feed is untrusted input. `gps_sim.py` is a faithful stand-in emitter for use when no hardware is present, and the console can spawn one per ROC automatically."));

// 10 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("10  HTTP API reference"));
c.push(P("JSON in, JSON out. Command endpoints are refused unless the console is armed, and every refusal names its reason."));
c.push(P("EVERY POST MUST BE LABELED `Content-Type: application/json` (a charset is fine), or it is refused 415 and logged. A web page on another site can make a browser send a form, multipart or text/plain POST without a preflight; a JSON label forces the browser to ask first, and the console answers no OPTIONS request and sends no Access-Control header, so that POST is never sent. The exceptions are the stops: `/api/cmd/stop`, `/api/cmd/pause`, and `/api/cmd/estop` when it latches are honored whatever they are labeled. An E-STOP body that cannot be read, is not an object, or does not say off LATCHES; only `on: false` or `on: 0` releases, and a release must be labeled JSON (`post_refusal`, `estop_wants_latch`)."));
c.push(TBL(["Endpoint", "Method", "Purpose"], [
  ["`/api/state`", "GET", "Current state snapshot"],
  ["`/events`", "GET (SSE)", "Live state stream"],
  ["`/api/vessel`", "GET / POST", "Active vessel configuration; POST switches vessel (gated disarmed + idle)"],
  ["`/api/vessels`", "GET", "Available vessel configurations"],
  ["`/api/connect`, `/api/disconnect`", "POST", "Attach or detach the VCU link"],
  ["`/api/cmd/arm`, `/api/cmd/estop`", "POST", "Arm gate and command e-stop"],
  ["`/api/cmd/upload`, `/api/cmd/start`, `/api/cmd/pause`, `/api/cmd/stop`", "POST", "Run control"],
  ["`/api/cmd/goto`, `/api/cmd/rth`, `/api/cmd/transit`, `/api/cmd/hold`", "POST", "Behaviors"],
  ["`/api/cmd/hold_clear`", "POST", "The hold disc re-certified while she holds: `hold_clear_m` (and an optional `note`) changes the radius she may drive straight back within, and nothing else. Refused in words unless armed, not e-stopped, the run under way and the vessel station-keeping"],
  ["`/api/cmd/sethome`, `/api/cmd/spawn`, `/api/cmd/reset`, `/api/cmd/energy`", "POST", "Home, simulator placement, power-cycle, energy"],
  ["`/api/roc`", "GET / POST", "ROC registry, lifecycle, GPS attachment, HOME selection, position push"],
  ["`/api/mission`", "GET / POST", "Persisted mission plan, with its revision `rev`. A POST that is not a plan is a 400 and one made to an older revision a 409 carrying the revision on disk; neither writes."],
  ["`/api/enc`, `/api/chartinfo`", "GET", "Chart features and the cell / confidence under the vessel"],
  ["`/api/waterlevel`, `/api/tide`, `/api/env`", "GET / POST", "Water level, tide, environment"],
  ["`/api/water/along?pts=LAT,LON;...&t0=&t1=`", "GET", "The lowest water above chart datum at each point between two times, from that point's own stations (a passage's section, while she sails it - step 6), each station's prediction lowered where NOAA's STOFS surge forecast runs under it (a point it set carries src stofs and tide_low_m; the answer a stofs block); a bad query is a 400 in words"],
  ["`/api/ais`", "GET", "Nearby traffic, proxied from the AIS service"],
  ["`/api/logs`, `/api/logevent`", "GET / POST", "Session recordings and event injection"],
], [3400, 1500, 4460]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("`/api/roc` takes an `op` field: `add`, `update`, `offset`, `motion`, `confirm`, `hold`, `gps_attach`, `gps_detach`, `remove`, `select_home`, `clear_home`, `feed`. Every op returns the fresh snapshot so a client stays in sync without a second request. `feed` is the external position push — any GPS bridge or ship navigation system can post to it."));

// 11 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("11  Data formats and persistence"));
c.push(TBL(["File", "Format", "Contents"], [
  ["`mission.json`", "JSON", "The committed plan: waypoints, survey lines, speed, arrival radius, completion mode, buffer"],
  ["`roc_config.json`", "JSON", "ROC definitions, placements and lifecycle state — capped at the 3 most recent (ROC_PERSIST_MAX), HOME always retained; enforced on save AND load, so an oversized file heals itself"],
  ["`vessels/<id>.json`", "JSON", "One vessel's complete configuration (chapter 4)"],
  ["`logs/*.jsonl`", "JSONL", "Append-only session recording: every command, setting, state transition and telemetry sample"],
  ["`charts/`", "Tiles + JSON", "Chart tile and ENC feature caches, regenerated at runtime"],
], [2300, 1400, 5660]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("Recordings are the input to the playback view, which replays a session read-only on the same chart: the vessel drives its recorded track, plans and commanded routes appear as they were sent, and a timeline lists every command and state transition including the refused ones."));
c.push(P("NOTHING IN THE CONSOLE DELETES A RECORDING OR A CACHED CHART. Shown the figures the measurement below produced, the operator decided (2026-09-15) to have recordings over thirty days compressed and to leave the chart cache uncapped. `compress_old_logs` gzips a session recording older than `STORAGE_OLD_DAYS` in place, on the storage thread - at least one per pass and then up to `LOG_COMPRESS_BUDGET_S`, never the session being recorded, and never a file in `logs/` that is not a recording. The original is removed only after the copy has been written, read back and compared byte for byte; any failure leaves the recording as it was and is printed. The record keeps its name and its mtime, so `list_logs` lists it once under its `.jsonl` name at the size from the gzip trailer with `on_disk` beside it, `safe_log_path` resolves that name to the `.gz`, and `read_log_text` decompresses it - `/api/log` and the playback view are unchanged, and any gzip tool opens the file. `--no-log-compress` turns it off, and the state carries `log_compress` so the report and the banner say which policy is actually in force. A pass that did anything is announced and written to the session log as `logs_compressed`."));
c.push(P("`StorageWatch` - started by `main()`, never at import - measures, at start and every `STORAGE_CHECK_S`: what `logs/` and `charts/` hold and what they occupy, priced by the allocation unit of the drive each folder's real path is on (a folder that is a link or junction is measured where it leads; a link inside the tree is not walked); the part of `logs/` older than `STORAGE_OLD_DAYS`; and the free space of each drive the plan, the session log and the chart cache are written to, grouped by device and named by where the files are. The tightest of those drives is published as `storage` in the state. Under `STORAGE_LOW_MB` it is low: the report goes to stderr, the session log records `storage_low` (and `storage_ok` on recovery), and the page raises DISK SPACE LOW once and keeps a DISK LOW pill on the top bar while it lasts - the banner slot is shared, and in the live check the chart's own banner took it four seconds later. When there is room again the pill comes down, and so does the banner if it is still its own."));

// 12 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("12  Configuration and constants"));
c.push(P("Vessel-specific values are NOT listed here — they live in the vessel file (chapter 4). What follows is console-level."));
c.push(TBL(["Constant", "Default", "Meaning"], [
  ["Web port", "8791", "Console UI"],
  ["`NOGO_RADIUS_M`", "5000 m", "Operating-area half-extent; re-extract after 0.6 × travel"],
  ["`NOGO_MIN_DEPTH_M`", "vessel-derived", "Draft + under-keel clearance; water shallower is nogo"],
  ["`NOGO_BUFFER_M`", "vessel-derived", "Shared keep-clear buffer, operator-adjustable"],
  ["`AIS_SHOW_RADIUS_KM`", "9.26 km (5 nm)", "The traffic RANGE — a true range circle from the vessel, clamped to the collect radius; `--ais-radius-km`, and live from the card. Not display alone: the clearance guard's AIS keep-outs are built from the same contacts, and need far less (a 45 s look-ahead). The card reads and sets it in NAUTICAL MILES, to a tenth below 10 nm (the default reads 5); the wire and the command-line flags stay metric"],
  ["`AIS_COLLECT_RADIUS_KM`", "150 km", "What the upstream subscription actually covers. Collect WIDE, filter NARROW — the display radius moves freely inside this and never re-subscribes"],
  ["AIS sources", "`auto`", "`--ais-source`, passed to the service's `--source`: any comma list of aisstream / digitraffic / aishub / nmea / opencpn. ALL enabled sources merge into ONE MMSI-keyed registry — per-vessel `srcs` records every reporting feed, `src` names the feed whose position is displayed, and a polled report older than the held position is dropped (static fields still merge). `--ais-nmea udp:PORT|tcp:HOST:PORT` (repeatable) adds local AIVDM endpoints, each with its own named health; `--ais-opencpn [HOST:]PORT` reads OpenCPN's TCP relay; aishub polls at its 1/min limit and needs `$AISHUB_USER` (membership = contributing a feed). Naming an endpoint enables its source"],
  ["Telemetry rate", "4 Hz", "Engine tick and SSE push"],
  ["Telemetry stale after", "2 s", "No frame while the console says it streams: the page says TELEMETRY STALE (6.3)"],
  ["`WATER_STALE_S`", "25 min", "A water level older than this is shown but not applied to charted depths (7.2)"],
  ["Current recomputed", "60 s", "`CurrentsMonitor.SAMPLE_S`; the cycle itself is looked for every 15 min (5.3)"],
  ["Stream sources looked for", "15 min, or a 15 km move", "`StreamSources.POLL_S`; station tables held a week ahead, a PacIOOS window one day (5.3)"],
  ["Fault clears after", "8 clean ticks / frames", "Two seconds without a failure, on the console's loop and on the page alike (5.1, 6.3)"],
  ["`BUILD_RECHECK_S`", "5 s", "How often the console looks at its own files for a new version on disk (6.4)"],
  ["`STORAGE_LOW_MB`", "2048 MB", "Less free than this on a drive the plan, the session log or the chart cache is written to raises DISK SPACE LOW (11). Nothing is ever deleted"],
  ["`LOG_COMPRESS` / `LOG_COMPRESS_BUDGET_S`", "on / 30 s", "Compression of recordings older than `STORAGE_OLD_DAYS`, and how long one pass may spend on it — at least one recording per pass either way. `--no-log-compress` turns it off, and the state's `log_compress` says which is in force (11)"],
  ["`STORAGE_OLD_DAYS` / `STORAGE_CHECK_S`", "30 days / 30 min", "The part of `logs/` older than this is counted apart in the storage readout, which is measured this often — and a recording older than it is compressed in place (11)"],
  ["Turn arc sampling", "~3 m", "Waypoint spacing along a generated survey turn"],
  ["ROC link thresholds", "5 s / 15 s", "Fresh → stale → lost for a live-fed ROC"],
  ["Display units", "METRIC", "The UNITS pill, METRIC or SAE (stored as km / nm): LONG distances read km or nm and fuel liters or US gallons, operator's stored choice, applied at the display edge by one formatter each (`fmtDist`, `fmtVol`). Canonical values are meters everywhere; short distances, depths and the LINES table stay metric, chart tiles stay in feet, the AIS card stays in nm"],
], [2600, 1900, 4860]));

// 13 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("13  Development practice and verification"));
c.push(H2("13.1  Regression harnesses"));
c.push(P("The regression suites guard behavior that has bitten repeatedly. None needs a third-party dependency; several stand up a REAL console and drive it over the API, because accepting a command proves nothing about whether it reached the vessel. The pre-commit hook runs every one of them unless everything staged is Markdown or git metadata, stops any that hangs after SUITE_LIMIT_S, and names the code to look at for each that fails."));
c.push(P("THE LIST BELOW IS READ FROM `tests/` AT BUILD TIME. It used to be a hand-written table introduced by the word “Fourteen”, and by the time anyone counted there were twenty. The descriptions are still written by hand — “what this guards” is prose no parser can invent — but the list and the count come from the filesystem, so a suite added tomorrow appears here without anyone remembering to add it."));
c.push(TBL(["Harness", "Guards"], harnessRows(), [2700, 6660]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("Enable the hook once per clone with `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`."));
c.push(H2("13.2  How these harnesses work, and how they break"));
c.push(P("The browser-side harnesses EXTRACT the real functions out of `static/asv.html` by brace matching and run them in Node against synthetic worlds. This tests the shipping code rather than a copy — but it also means the harness ENUMERATES the functions it extracts, so it breaks silently when the code under test gains a new dependency. Treat “harness crashed” exactly as loudly as “check failed”: a suite that cannot run protects nothing, and a suite that prints success while testing dead code is worse than none at all."));
c.push(H2("13.3  Tests with teeth"));
c.push(P("A check that cannot fail is not a check. Every harness in this repository has been verified by MUTATION: deliberately breaking the behavior under test and confirming the specific assertions fail. Where a test asserts that something is refused, it is paired with a case proving the same input is ACCEPTED under valid conditions — otherwise a function that refused everything would pass."));
c.push(H2("13.4  What static checks cannot catch"));
c.push(P("A clearance check on a PLANNED path does not catch FOLLOW OVERSHOOT. An idealised arc can validate perfectly clear and still be tighter than the vessel can hold, in which case the vessel overshoots outboard — into the very keep-out the arc was hugging. Turn geometry must therefore be verified with a live simulation run sampling vessel-versus-nogo at zero buffer, not with geometry alone. This is why the minimum turn radius exists."));

// 14 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("14  Known limitations and roadmap"));
c.push(B("`RealVcu` command and telemetry codecs are unimplemented by design. The console is a simulator; the real link refuses honestly rather than pretending."));
c.push(B("The simulator caps yaw RATE, not radius, so the modeled minimum turn radius grows linearly with speed. Real hulls hold a roughly constant minimum radius. The cap is calibrated at survey speed, which is where surveys are planned, and is pessimistic at high speed."));
c.push(B("Raw API command and upload calls with no supplied route BYPASS chart-aware routing, because routing is client-side. Rehearse through the interface, or place raw waypoints in open water."));
c.push(B("Chart scale and currency vary. The console is a planning AID and says so in a banner; it is not hydrographically certified, and the operator remains responsible for the plan."));
c.push(B("Traffic data comes from a crowd-sourced network with real coverage gaps. A quiet area is reported as “connected, no vessels reporting” rather than as an error, because those are different conditions."));

// 15 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("15  Extension guide"));
c.push(H2("15.1  Adding a vessel"));
c.push(P("Write `vessels/<id>.json`. No code changes. See chapter 4."));
c.push(H2("15.2  Adding a behavior"));
c.push(P("Add the command endpoint to the server, a method on `Engine` that builds a route and calls the shared run-route path, and a client function that plans the route against the nogo model before uploading it. Reuse the existing route search and channel lane rather than adding a parallel mechanism — a behavior that routes its own way will diverge from every other behavior the first time the router improves."));
c.push(H2("15.3  Adding a vessel-dependent value"));
c.push(P("Put the field in the vessel schema with a derived fallback so older vessel files still load, read it in `apply_vessel()` so a live switch re-derives it, mirror it to the client through `/api/vessel` if the browser needs it, and add a regression assertion that the value actually CHANGES between two different vessels. That last step is what catches a value that was wired up but never re-derived."));
c.push(H2("15.4  Adding UI"));
c.push(P("Give every interactive element a stable unique id (the multi-monitor bridge forwards by id), key handlers off class or `data-*`, register any new pop-out card in the bridged-selector list and the card-title map, and rebuild card rows only when the underlying SET changes — rebuilding on every telemetry frame will clobber a field the operator is typing into."));
c.push(H2("15.5  House rules"));
c.push(B("No hardcoded vessel constants. Ever. The vessel file is the single source of truth."));
c.push(B("Keep the console core vendor-neutral. Vessel files may name real vessels; the console may not."));
c.push(B("Never weaken the safety model: arm-gating, e-stop, link-loss failsafe and the refusal-with-a-reason contract."));
c.push(B("Update the relevant README in the same change as the behavior it documents, and rebuild this manual from its script."));

// ---- document --------------------------------------------------------------
write("asv-simulator-technical-manual.docx", c);
